    "ls|LS17_S9_U_315": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 315 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 08.08.2022 \n \nE-SHRAM PORTAL \n \n*315. SHRI MAGUNTA SREENIVASULU REDDY: \n \n  Will the Minister of LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT be pleased to \nstate:  \n \n(a)whether the e-Shram portal has crashed multiple times due to heavy \ntraffic therein; \n(b)if so, whether the Government has taken any steps to redress the \nsame and if so, the details thereof; \n(c)whether the Government has taken any steps to make the e -Shram \nportal differently-abled friendly, if so, the details thereof and if not, \nthe reasons therefor; and \n(d)whether the Government has any plans to link the e -Shram card \nwith other cards such as ‘One Nation One Ration’ card to make \ndelivery of benefits to workers more e fficient, if so, the details \nthereof and if not, the reasons therefor? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \n(SHRI BHUPENDER YADAV) \n \n(a) to (d): A statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n*                                                 ****** \n \n  \n\n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED  TO IN REPLY TO PART (a) TO (d) OF LOK \nSABHA STARRED QUESTION NO. 315 FOR 08.08.2022. REGARDING    \nE-SHRAM PORTAL BY SHRI MAGUNTA SREENIVASULU REDDY.  \n \n(a) to (d): On 26 th August, 2021 Ministry of Labour & Employment \nlaunched eShram  portal to create a National Database of the \nUnorganised Workers . The eShram portal was initially designed to \nhave a daily registration capacity  of 10 - 15 lakhs/day. However, the \nportal received unprecedented response from unorganised workers \nand the actual load increased beyond the initial designed capacity. To \naddress this issue, eShram portal capacity was increased to support \nregistrations up to 80 lakhs/day. \n \nThe differently abled assistive features of the portal \nenables people with visual impairments to access the website using \nassistive technologies, such as screen readers. A user with visual \ndisability can access this website using font magnifiers  and screen \nreaders. Further, Ministry has on -boarded more than 4 lakh Common \nService Centres for registration on eShram portal. \nOne of the main objectives of the eShram portal other than \nregistration is to facilitate delivery of benefit s of social security  \nschemes to the unorganised workers through integration with other \nGovernment portals. In the budget 202 2-23 Government announced \nintegration of eShram, with National Career Service (NCS) \nportal, ASEEM portal and Udyam portal to enable credit facilitation, \nskilling and recruitment with an aim to further formalise the economy \nand enhance entrepreneurial oppor tunities for all. The integration of \neShram has already been done with NCS portal. \n \n****** ",
    "ls|LS17_S9_U_316": "  \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \nLOK  SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 316 \n  ANSWERED ON 19/07/2022 \n \nDISCONTINUANCE OF MANUAL ATTENDANCE UNDER MGNREGS \n \n316.  SHRI V.K.SREEKANDAN: \n \nWill the Minister of RURAL DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether it is a fact that the Government has issued an order to discontinue manual \nattendance of Mahatma Gandhi National Rural Employment Guarantee Scheme \n(MGNREGS) work sites with more than 20 workers by replacing it  with a mobile \nphone based application; \n(b)  if so, the details thereof; \n(c)  whether it is a fact that many organizations have said that the said order is a violation \nof the MGNREGS law and also flagged a series of technical and sociological issues \nwith the app; \n(d)  whether it is a fact that the app has been designed completely in English and there is \nno technical help provided to redress problems; \n(e)  whether it is also a fact that having a Smartphone is now mandatory for mates to \nrecord attendance which most of them do not have the same; and \n(f)  if so, whether the Government is considering withdrawing the said order and if so, the \ndetails thereof? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT  \n(SADHVI NIRANJAN JYOTI) \n \n(a) & (b)  National Mobile Monitoring Software (NMMS) App is a big step towards bringing \ntransparency and proper monitoring of Mahatma Gandhi National Rural Employment \nGuarantee Scheme. Government of India has decided that, from May 16, 2022,   the workers' \nattendance will be captured only through the National Mobile Monitoring System (NMMS) \napp for all the worksites wherein muster rolls are issued for 20 or more beneficiaries. \n  \n(c)   There is no violation of the Mahatma Gandhi National Rural Employment Guarantee \nAct, 2005. \n  \n(d)    No, Sir. \n  \n(e)   There is a widespread availability of smartphones in the rural areas as per Annual \nStatus of Education Report (Rural) 2021  of ASER Centre,   which reported that 67.7% \nsurveyed rural households have at least one smartphone. Similar data has been reported by \nother agencies. Thus, it is not a major bottleneck. \n  \n(f)     No, Sir. \n***** ",
    "ls|LS17_S9_U_317": "  \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \nLOK  SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 317 \n  ANSWERED ON 19/07/2022 \n \nLANDLESS BENEFICIARIES UNDER PMAY-G \n \n317.  SHRI JAYADEV GALLA: \n \nWill the Minister of RURAL DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether the Government has taken any steps to  ensure that the genuine beneficiaries \nare not deprived of  the benefits under the Pradhan Mantri Awas Yojana -Gramin \n(PMAY-G); \n(b)  the number of days taken to build a house under the Yojana, State-wise; \n(c)  the number of beneficiaries yet to be provided land/house, State/UT-wise; \n(d)  the details of the houses built under the Yojana  along with the time taken to build \nthese houses and phase-wise funds disbursed, date-wise under PMAY-G; \n(e)  whether the Government has any plan to increase  per unit assistance amount under \nPMAY-G and if not, the reasons therefor; and \n(f)  the number of landless beneficiaries that are  covered under the PMAY -G, State/UT-\nwise? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT  \n(SADHVI NIRANJAN JYOTI) \n \n(a) Under Pradhan Mantri Awaas Yojana - Gramin (PMAY -G), the target is the  \n������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\nThe beneficiaries have been identified based on the housing deprivation parameters \nprescribed under Socio Economic Caste Census (SECC) 2011. After due verification by the \nGram Sabha and completion of Appellate Process, Gram Panchayat -wise Permanent Wait \nList ( PWL) is prepared. The auto generated priority list of households from SECC, 2011 \ndatabase was provided to the States/Union Territories (UTs) for conducting Gram Sabha \nmeetings to finalize the PWL. As on 13.07.2022, the number of eligible beneficiaries \navailable through SECC database stands at 2.15 crore (approx.). Further, the details of such \nhouseholds who, though eligible for inclusion in PWL, but claimed to be left out from the \nSECC 2011 based PWL, have been captured in the Awaas+ Survey. The Survey was \nconducted during January 2018 and March 2019. In this exercise, the States/UTs have \nuploaded details of additional households. The beneficiaries from finalised Awaas+ lists are \nalso being granted benefits under PMAY -G as per the approved guidelines, within  a ceiling \nof 2.95 crore houses. Thus, the Ministry has taken effective steps to ensure that genuine \nbeneficiaries are not left out of the scheme. \n  \n(b) & (d) The Framework for Implementation (FFI) of PMAY -G provides that the house \nconstruction should be c ompleted within 12 months of sanctioning of house to the \nbeneficiary. The State/UT -wise & Phase -wise details of houses built/constructed under the \nscheme, average number of days taken for completing the construction of the house and the \ntotal Central Assistance released since inception till 13.07.2022 is given in Annexure-I. \n  \n \n \n \n\n \n(c) & (f) The State/UT -wise number of landless beneficiaries, identified in the PWL of \nPMAY-G, those that have been provided land or assistance for purchase of land and ones that \nare remaining to be provided land is given in Annexure-II. \n  \n(e) At present, there is no proposal under consideration of the Ministry for increasing per unit \nassistance under the Scheme. \n \n*******  \n\nANNEXURE-I \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (b) AND ( d) OF LOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 317 TO BE ANSWERED ON 19.07.2022 REGARDING \nLANDLESS BENEFICIARIES UNDER PMAY-G \n  \nState/UT-wise & Phase-wise details of Central Assistance released, total number of houses completed \nand average completion time since inception till 13.07.2022. \n  \n  \nSl. \nNo. \n  \n  \nName of the \nState/UT \nPhase-I \n(2016-17 to 2018-19) \nPhase-II \n(2019-20 to 2022-23)  \n  \n  \nAverage \ncompletio\nn time  \n(in days) \n  \nCentral \nAssistance \nreleased \n(Rs in \ncrore) \nNumber of \nhouses \ncompleted under \nPMAY-G \n(units in Nos.) \nCentral \nAssistance \nreleased (Rs \nin crore) \nNumber of \nhouses \ncompleted under \nPMAY-G \n(units in Nos.) \n1 Arunachal Pradesh 66.23 4,407 104.85 553 170 \n2 Assam 3,235.68 2,32,422 8,708.50 3,48,736 359 \n3 Bihar 7,166.16 11,08,610 16,541.42 14,76,920 366 \n4 Chhattisgarh 6,100.18 7,53,083 869.67 72,128 277 \n5 Goa 2.85 133 0 0 468 \n6 Gujarat 1,580.11 1,99,030 12,65.63 1,82,816 309 \n7 Haryana 124.08 20,657 34.55 329 453 \n8 Himachal Pradesh 98.11 7,050 56.12 2,893 283 \n9 Jammu And Kashmir 356.98 30,893 1,248.42 60,559 387 \n10 Jharkhand 4,156.12 5,15,784 6,999.18 7,02,518 304 \n11 Kerala 121.90 20,037 35.14 235 321 \n12 Madhya Pradesh 1,0827.84 13,67,996 11,956.07 1,22,0176 235 \n13 Maharashtra 2,973.27 4,04,568 4,375.22 4,24,988 310 \n14 Manipur 120.53 9,716 196.71 5,311 451 \n15 Meghalaya 249.73 20,106 303.80 9,960 472 \n16 Mizoram 60.51 5,511 58.07 3 337 \n17 Nagaland 55.09 4,239 34.80 0 418 \n18 Odisha ,7908.91 9,44,555 6,031.07 7,50,658 258 \n19 Punjab 91.61 13,799 67.52 9,370 281 \n20 Rajasthan 5,107.33 6,75,758 6,013.83 6,38,911 268 \n21 Sikkim 11.91 1,070 1.22 0 331 \n22 Tamil Nadu 2,041.88 2,64,119 1,495.07 1,00,833 316 \n23 Tripura 325.38 2,4591 1,711.61 70976 354 \n24 Uttar Pradesh 9,963.73 1,26,6642 9,703.54 1,30,7390 195 \n25 Uttarakhand 184.64 12,438 149.17 9,224 292 \n26 West Bengal 10,323.15 13,73,944 15,474.38 19,57,630 261 \n27 Andaman And \nNicobar \n2.29 \n653 \n16.88 464 \n243 \n28 Dadra And Nagar \nHaveli \n  \n15.61 2,138 \n55.98 0 \n438 \n29 Daman And Diu 0.59 13 0 0 83 \n30 Lakshadweep 0.71 44 0 0 771 \n31 Puducherry@ - - - - - \n32 Andhra Pradesh^ 755.11 46,707 0 0 NA \n33 Karnataka^ 1,059.91 95,640 309.60 5,990 NA \n34 Telangana@ 190.79 - - - - \n35 Ladakh*  0.0 1,228 0 201 206 \n  Total 75,278.92 94,27,581 93,818.02 93,59,772 272 \n������������������������������������