    "ls|LS17_S9_U_340": " \nO.I.H. \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF FISHERIES, ANIMAL HUSBANDRY & DAIRYING \nDEPARTMENT OF FISHERIES \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. 340 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 19th July, 2022 \n \nPromotion of Fisheries in Rajasthan \n \n340.  SHRI BALAK NATH: \n \nWill the Minister of FISHERIES, ANIMAL HUSBANDRY AND DAIRYING \nमत्स्यपालन, पशुपालन और डेयरी मंत्री be pleased to state: \n(a) whether the  Government is taking any steps to promote fisheries in Rajasthan including \nAlwar district and if so, the details thereof; \n(b) whether any funds have been allocated for promoting fisheries in the State of Rajasthan \nincluding Alwar district during the last three years; and  \n(c) if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF FISHERIES, ANIMAL HUSBANDRY AND DAIRYING \n \n                                                                                   (SHRI PARSHOTTAM RUPALA) \n \n \n(a) to (c): The Department of Fisheries, Ministry of Fisheries, Animal Husbandry and Dairying \nhas been implementing schemes for development and promotion of fisheries in all the states  \nand Union Territories including in the state of Rajasthan and schemes implemented include; (i) \nCentrally Sponsored Scheme on Blue Revolution: Integrated Development and Management of \nFisheries at a total outlay of Rs 3000 crore for a period of 5 years from 2015 -16 and 2019-20  \n(ii) Fisheries and Aquaculture Infrastructure Development Fund (FIDF) with a total fund size \nof Rs 7522.48 crore from 2018-19 to 2023-24 for providing concessional finance (iii) Pradhan \nMantri Matsya Sampada Yojana (PMMSY) as a part of Atmanirbhar Bharat Abhiyan with an \ninvestment of Rs.20, 050 crore for a period of 5 years with effect from 2020 -21 to 2024-25 in \nall the States/Union Territories including in the state of Rajasthan.  Further, from the year 2018-\n2019, Kisan Credit Card (KCC) facility ha s also been extended to fisher s and fish farmers to \nhelp them to meet their working capital requirements.  \n \n \nDetails of the central funds released to the Government of Rajasthan by the Department \nof Fisheries, Government of India during the last three years for development and promotion \nof fisheries in the State are as below: \n \n \nSl. No Name of the Scheme Financial \nYear \nCentral funds \nreleased (Rs. \nIn lakhs) \n1 Centrally Sponsored Scheme on Blue \nRevolution: Integrated Development and \nManagement of Fisheries \n2019-20 266.20 \n2 Pradhan Mantri Matsya Sampada Yojana \n(PMMSY) \n2020-21 \n \n \n210.82 \n3 Pradhan Mantri Matsya Sampada Yojana \n(PMMSY) \n2021-22 175.65 \n  Total 652.67                                 \n                                                             ******            \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S9_U_341": "  \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \nLOK  SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 341 \n  ANSWERED ON 19/07/2022 \n \nCONSTRUCTION OF ANGANWADI CENTRES UNDER MGNREGS \n \n341.  SHRI JUAL ORAM: \n \nWill the Minister of RURAL DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether construction of Anganwadi centres under  Mahatma Gandhi National Rural \nEmployment Guarantee Scheme (MGNREGS) is permissible; \n(b)  if so, the number of Anganwadi centres  constructed under MGNREGS in Odisha \nduring each of the last five years and the current year, district-wise; and \n(c)  the number of Anganwadi centres that the  Government is planning to construct under \nMGNREGS during the current year and during 2022-23 in Odisha, district-wise? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT  \n(SADHVI NIRANJAN JYOTI) \n \n(a):    As per Para 4 (I) of Schedule –I of the Mahatma Gandhi National Rural Employment \nGuarantee Act (Mahatma Gandhi NREGA), construction of buildings for Anganwadi Centres \nat the village level or block level is permissible under Mahatma Gandhi National Rural \nEmployment Guarantee Scheme (Mahatma Gandhi NREGS). \n \n(b)  Year-wise and district-wise details of Anganwadi Centers constructed under Mahatma \nGandhi NREGS in the State of  Odisha during the last five financial years 2017 -18, 2018-19, \n2019-20, 2020-21, 2021-22  and current financial year 2022-23 (as on 13.07.2022) is given at \nAnnexure. \n  \n(c):     Mahatma Gandhi National Rural Employment Guarantee Scheme (Mahatma Gandhi \nNREGS) is demand driven wage employment programme with bottom up planning. The \nMinistry does not plan or set any target for any specific work (including Anganwadi Centres) \nunder the Scheme for any States/Uts. The Gram Panchayat is responsible for identification  of \nworks in the Gram Panchayat area to be taken under the Scheme as per recommendations of \nthe Gram Sabha, and for executing and supervising such works.  \n  \n******  \n\n \nAnnexure referred in reply to parts (b) of Lok Sabha Unstarred Question No.341 dated \n19.07.2022 \n  \nYear-wise and district -wise details of A nganwadi Centers constructed  under Mahatma \nGandhi NREGS in the State of Odisha during the last five financial years 2017 -18, 2018-19, \n2019-20, 2020-21, 2021-22  and current financial year 2022-23 (as on 13.07.2022). \nS. No. District  2022-23  2021-22 2020-21 2019-20 2018-19  2017-18 \n1 ANGUL 6 37 23 41 55 59 \n2 BALESHWAR 38 103 183 114 133 131 \n3 BARGARH 26 150 146 139 125 151 \n4 BHADRAK 27 48 73 49 12 40 \n5 BOLANGIR 6 62 47 99 100 152 \n6 BOUDH 3 11 5 26 27 41 \n7 CUTTACK 39 172 62 131 78 120 \n8 DEOGARH 15 41 15 41 20 44 \n9 DHENKANAL 5 28 73 88 81 184 \n10 GAJAPATI 15 65 51 68 117 171 \n11 GANJAM 18 84 54 158 171 288 \n12 JAGATSINGHAPUR 8 37 35 58 31 34 \n13 JAJPUR 34 100 79 156 118 101 \n14 JHARSUGUDA 8 18 18 60 24 26 \n15 KALAHANDI 11 63 27 100 68 55 \n16 KANDHAMAL 15 118 73 38 32 91 \n17 KENDRAPARA 6 124 59 92 97 65 \n18 KENDUJHAR 6 68 44 154 124 166 \n19 KHORDHA 11 100 27 51 57 69 \n20 KORAPUT 8 181 102 272 166 101 \n21 MALKANGIRI 1 23 8 10 16 25 \n22 MAYURBHANJ 8 26 46 127 187 406 \n23 NABARANGAPUR 7 54 42 140 55 99 \n24 NAYAGARH 12 88 85 87 42 39 \n25 NUAPADA 7 15 5 15 8 21 \n26 PURI 61 125 54 95 63 73 \n27 RAYAGADA 12 63 35 85 48 132 \n28 SAMBALPUR 9 6 9 37 20 97 \n29 SONEPUR 8 24 36 53 72 189 \n30 SUNDARGARH 4 36 56 102 81 134 \n  Total 434 2070 1572 2686 2228 3304 \n           \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S9_U_342": "O.I.H.\n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \n \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \nLOK SABHA \n \n \nUN\nSTARRED QUESTION NO. \n342\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE \n19\nth\n \nJULY\n, 2022\n \n \nIMPROVING RURAL AGRICULTURAL MARKETS\n \n342. \n \nSHRIMATI KESHARI \nDEVI PATEL:\n \nSHRI KANAKMAL KATARA:\n \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS\n \nWELFARE \nकृ\u0000ष\n \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \nमं\u0000ी\n \nbe pleased to state:\n \n \n(a) \n \nthe details of the steps being taken by the\n \nGovernment to improve rural \nagricultural markets in the\n \ncountry, State\n-\nwise including Uttar Pradesh and\n \nRajasthan \nespecially Banswara\n-\nDungarpur districts of\n \nRajasthan; and\n \n \n(b) \n \nthe various guidelines issued for ensuring\n \nremunerative price to the farmers \nfor their produce?\n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS\n \nWELFARE\n \nकृ\u0000ष\n \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \nमं\u0000ी\n \n \n   \n(SHRI NARENDRA SINGH\n \nTOMAR)\n \n(a):\n \n \nThe development an\nd upgradation of physical infrastructure of rural haats \nunder control of Panchayats through States/ Union Territories is undertaken through \nMahatma Gandhi National Rural Employment Guarantee Scheme (MGNREGS) as \nongoing process for development of GrAMs.\n \nAs \nper the information received from Ministry of Rural Development, the \nState/\nUnion Territory (\nUT\n)\n-\nwise (including Uttar Pradesh and Rajasthan) details of \nvillage haats works under \nMGNREGS\n \nas on 13\nth\n \nJuly, 2022\n \nis at Annexure\n-\nI. \nFurther, \ni\nn the State of Rajas\nthan,\n \nas on 13\nth\n \nJuly, 2022,\n \nthree\n \nvillage haats have \n \ndeveloped \nin\n \nBanswara District while work is in progress in three village haats\n.\n \nFurthermore, \nthree village haats have been developed in Dungarpur district while work is in \nprogress in one village haat\n.  \n \n(b)\n \n \n   \n \nImproving infrastructure and facilities in rural agricultural markets and also \nservices thereof with efficient operation and management is critical for ensuring \nremunerative price to the farmers for their produce. Therefore, the Department of \nAgriculture and Farmers Welfare(DA&FW) has circulated the Operational Guidelines \n\nto the states / UTs to avail the assistance for developing marketing and farm level \nprocessing infrastructure in the rural agricultural markets.\n \n \nIn addition, DA&FW has \nalso c\nirculated Standard Operating Procedure (SOP) for operation and management \nof rural agricultural markets.\n \nFurther, the Government of India has launched \nNational \nAgriculture Market (e\n-\nNAM) \nwhich \nis a virtual platform integrating physical wholesale \nmandis/ ma\nrkets of different States/ Union Territories (UTs) to facilitate online trading \nof agricultural commodities through transparent price discovery method to enable \nfarmers to realize better remunerative prices for their produce.\n \nT\nhe DA&FW has \ncirculated the O\nperational Guidelines to the states / UTs to avail the assistance\n \nunder e\n-\nNAM scheme. \n \n \n\n \n                                                                                               \nAnnexure\n-\nI\n \n \n \n \nState/UT\n-\nwise (including Uttar Pradesh and Rajasthan) det\nails of village \nhaats works under Mahatma Gandhi NREGS as on 13\nth\n \nJuly, 2022\n \nS \nNo.\n \nState/UT\n \nOngoing\n \nCompleted\n \nNumber Of Works\n \nNumber Of Works\n \n1\n \nANDHRA PRADESH\n \n79\n \n47\n \n2\n \nARUNACHAL PRADESH\n \n8\n \n7\n \n3\n \nASSAM\n \n42\n \n23\n \n4\n \nBIHAR\n \n70\n \n27\n \n5\n \nCHHATTISGARH\n \n178\n \n156\n \n6\n \nGUJA\nRAT\n \n27\n \n3\n \n7\n \nHARYANA\n \n0\n \n1\n \n8\n \nHIMACHAL PRADESH\n \n66\n \n66\n \n9\n \nJAMMU AND KASHMIR\n \n24\n \n32\n \n10\n \nJHARKHAND\n \n1\n \n0\n \n11\n \nKARNATAKA\n \n50\n \n25\n \n12\n \nKERALA\n \n32\n \n18\n \n14\n \nMADHYA PRADESH\n \n151\n \n201\n \n16\n \nMANIPUR\n \n70\n \n86\n \n17\n \nMEGHALAYA\n \n18\n \n13\n \n18\n \nMIZORAM\n \n53\n \n285\n \n19\n \nNAGALAND\n \n14\n \n4\n \n20\n \nODISHA\n \n232\n \n90\n \n21\n \nPUNJAB\n \n0\n \n9\n \n22\n \nRAJASTHAN\n \n48\n \n58\n \n23\n \nSIKKIM\n \n1\n \n3\n \n24\n \nTAMIL NADU\n \n64\n \n64\n \n25\n \nTELANGANA\n \n66\n \n15\n \n26\n \nTRIPURA\n \n1\n \n10\n \n27\n \nUTTAR PRADESH\n \n244\n \n120\n \n28\n \nUTTARAKHAND\n \n25\n \n27\n \n29\n \nWEST BENGAL\n \n210\n \n145\n \n30\n \nPUDUCHERRY\n \n0\n \n1\n \n \n \nTotal\n \n1774\n \n1536\n \n \n*****\n ",
    "ls|LS17_S9_U_343": " \n GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 343 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 19TH JULY, 2022/ASHADHA 28, 1944 (SAKA) \n \nINCREASE IN DRUG ABUSE AMONG YOUTH \n \n343.  SHRI K. MURALEEDHARAN: \n \nWill the Minister of HOME AFFAIRS be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether the consumption of drugs and narcotic  substances like heroin, \ncannabis and other opioids has  increased significantly in the country and if \nso, the details thereof; \n \n(b) whether it is true that the consumption of drugs  among the youth is \nincreasing and now roughly around  10 crore (100 million) Indians consume \ndrugs whereas 15  years ago it was around 2 crore (20 million) and if so, the  \ndetails thereof; \n \n(c) whether the Government has any plan to combat  drug abuse among th e \nyouth in the country; and \n \n(d) if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n(SHRI NITYANAND RAI) \n(a) & (b) :  The Ministry of Social Justice and Empowerment has conducted \n‘Comprehensive National Survey on Extent and Pattern of Substance Use in \nIndia’ through the National Drug Dependence Treatment Centre (NDDTC) of \nAll India Institute of Medical Sciences (AIIMS), New Delhi during 2018. The \nreport of the survey was released in February, 2019.  \n \n \n\n --2-- \nL.S.US.Q.NO. 343 FOR 19.07.2022 \n \nAs per the said report the details of major drug abuse are as under :- \nS.No Name of the \nSubstance  \nAge 10-17 years Age 18-75 years \nEstimated No. of users Estimated No. of users \n1 Cannabis 20,00,000 2,90,18,000 \n2 Opioids  40,00,000 1,86,44,000 \n3 Cocaine  2,00,000 9,40,000 \n \n(c) & (d) The Government of India through Ministry of Social Justice & \nEmpowerment has been implementing National Action Plan for Drug Demand \nReduction (NAPDDR) scheme under which financial assistance is provided to \n(i) State Governments/Union Territory(UT) Administrations for preventive \nEducation and Awareness Generation, Capacity Building, Skill development , \nvocational training and livelihood support of ex -drug addicts. (ii)  Non-\nGovernmental Organisations  (NGOs)/Voluntary Organizations  (VOs) for \nrunning and maintenance of Integrated Rehabilitation Centres for Addicts \n(IRCAs), Community based peer Led Interve ntion (CPLI) for early detection of \nDrug Use among Adolescents and Outreach and Drop In Centres (ODIC), \nDistrict De -Addiction Centres (DDACs) and Addiction treatment facilities \n(ATFs) in Government Hospitals. \nIn addition, the Government of India is taking sustained and coordinated \naction for tackling the problem of substance abuse among the youth. This \nincludes:  \n \n\n--3-- \nL.S.US.Q.NO. 343 FOR 19.07.2022 \n(i) Launching of Nasha Mukt Bharat Abhiyaan (NMBA) in 272 most \nvulnerable districts with a massive community outreach programme, \ninvolving more than 8000 youth volunteers. More than 3.3 crore youth \nhave been reached out under the Abhiyaan.  \n(ii) Maintaining of 350 Integrated Rehabilitation Centres , which  not \nonly treat the drug addicts but also pr ovides motivational \ncounseling,  after care  services and re-integration into the social \nmainstream.  \n(iii) Maintaining of 53 Community base d Peer Led Intervention (CPLI) \nCentres with focus on vulnerable and at risk children and \nadolescents. Under this, peer educ ators engage children for \nawareness generation and life skill activities.  \n(iv) Setting up and maintaining of 73 Outreach and Drop In Centres \n(ODIC) to provide safe and secure space for substance users with \nprovision of screening, assessment and counseling and thereafter \nprovide referral and linkage for treatment and rehabilitation \nservices of substance dependent.  \n(v) Setting up of 38 Addiction Treatment Facilities (ATFs) in \nGovernment hospitals. \n(vi) The Ministry of Social Justice and Empowerment is also running a \nNational Toll Free Helpline 14446 to provide tele -counseling to \nDrug users and refer them to the nearest de-addiction centre.  \n(vii) Narcotics Control Bureau ( NCB) has coordinated with Ministry of \nSocial Justice & Empowerment (MoSJ&E) and National Commission \nfor Protection of Child Rights (NCPCR) to spread awareness against \ndrug abuse and protect the youth, especially children from falling \nprey  to  the  drug   menace  by  formulating  a  Joint  Action  Plan  on  \n \n \n\n--4-- \nL.S.US.Q.NO. 343 FOR 19.07.2022 \n \nPrevention of Drugs and Substance s Use among Children and Illicit \nTrafficking.  \n \n(viii) NCB has also launched Awareness campaigns through various social \nmedia platforms by way of Audio Video messages of eminent \npersonalities from the field of Politics, Bureaucracy, Sports, Films, \nMusic etc and also through Telecom service providers, FM radios, \nTelevision Channels, etc. \n(ix) Anti drugs messages were released from the twitter handles of \neminent personalities. \n \n***** ",
    "ls|LS17_S9_U_344": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nLOK SABHA\n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 344\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 19\nTH\n \nJULY, 2022\n \n \nDELAY IN KHARIF SOWING \n \n \n344.  SHRI ADHIKARI DEEPAK (DEV):\n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \n \nक ृ िष\n \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \nमं\u0000ी\n \nbe pleased to state:\n \n(a) whether the Government is aware that Kharif sowing has been delayed this year and if \nso, the reasons for the delay;\n \n(b) the details about the Kharif\n \nsowing that has been completed so far;\n \n(c) whether it is true that farmers are moving away from pulses and foodgrains to cotton etc.; \nand\n \n(d) if so, the impact of such move on food security in the country?\n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nTHE MINISTER OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS \nWELFARE\n \nक ृ िष\n \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \nमं\u0000ी\n             \n(SHRI NARENDRA SINGH TOMAR)\n \n \n(a)\n \nto (d\n) \nAs per the reports available from the states\n,\n \nsowing \nhas\n \ncommenced on time in \nmajor parts of the country\n. \nA\nrea \nsown \nunder \nKharif \ncrops \nreported \nas on \n15\n.07.2022\n \nis \n592.11\n \nlakh \nh\nectares\n \ncompared to last year corresponding period 591.30 lakh hectares\n.\n \n \nDepartment\n \nof Agriculture & Farmers Welfare \nis \nfocusing \nt\no increase the production\n \nand \nproductivity \nof \ndiversified crops of \ncereals\n, \npulses\n, \noilseeds\n, \ncotton\n, \nsugarcane\n, \njute \n& \nmesta\n, \nhorticulture crops \netc., under the various schemes viz, \n(National Food Security \nMission (\nNFSM\n)\n, \nMission on Integrated Development of Horticulture (\nMIDH\n)\n, \n \nRashtriya \nKrishi Vikas Yojana \n-\n \nRemunerative approaches for Agriculture and \nAllied Sector \nRejuvenation \n(\nRKVY\n-\nRAFTAAR)\n \netc\n.\n \n \n*****\n ",
    "ls|LS17_S9_U_345": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF PANCHAYATI RAJ \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 345 \nANSWERED ON-19.07.2022 \n \nDIGITAL MAPPING OF VILLAGES \n  \n345. SHRI BALASHOWRY VALLABHANENI: \n  \nWill the Minister of PANCHAYATI RAJ be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether it is a true fact that the Government is thinking to prepare digital maps of all 6 \nlakh villages in the country; \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n \n(c) the difference between digital mapping of villages and SVAMITVA; and \n \n(d) whether any guidelines have been issued in this regard to and if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR PANCHAYATI RAJ  \n(SHRI KAPIL MORESHWAR PATIL) \n \n(a) and (b) Yes Sir. The Central Sector Scheme SVAMITVA, aims to provide the ‘Record of \nRights’ to village household owners possessing houses in inhabited areas, called Abadi in some \nStates, with legal ownership rights (Property cards/Title deeds). Under the scheme, the land \nparcels in rural inhabited area of all the villages are surveyed using drone technology. The \nscheme aims to cover 6.62 l akh villages in the country from Financial Year 2020 -21 to \nFinancial Year 2024-25. It is being implemented with the collaborative efforts of the Ministry \nof Panchayati Raj, Survey of India (SoI), State Revenue Department, State Panchayati Raj \nDepartment and National Informatics Centre. \n \n(c) Digital mapping of village is to prepare digital maps by the use of advance technology \nto give accurate representations of village area.  Under SVAMITVA scheme rural inhabited \narea, called Abadi in some States, are mapped by Survey of India using drone. It generates high \nresolution, geo-referenced maps to confer ownership property rights.  \n \n(d)  Detailed Guidelines / Framework for implementation of SVAMITVA scheme has been \nissued and can be accessed at the Ministry of Pan chayati Raj website \n(https://www.panchayat.gov.in/) and SVAMITVA scheme website (https://svamitva.nic.in/). \n \n*** ",
    "ls|LS17_S9_U_346": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF PANCHAYATI RAJ \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 346 \nANSWERED ON 19.07.2022 \n \nFACILITIES TO PANCHAYATS \n \n346. SHRIMATI APARAJITA SARANGI: \n \nWill the Minister of PANCHAYATI RAJ be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government has reviewed basic facilities provided to the Panchayats in \nOdisha for better management of rains and floods and if so, the details thereof; \n \n(b) the data for the number of women in Odisha who are members of panchayats and the \ndata for women sarpanch;  \n \n(c) whether the Government has extended support to the panchayats in Odisha to foster \ntheir participation in e-Panchayat Mission Mode Project, if so, the details thereof and \nthe number of panchayats from Odisha currently part of the same; and  \n \n(d) whether SVAMITVA scheme has been implemented in Odisha and the e -property \ncards issued thereunder and if so, the details thereof?  \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR PANCHAYATI RAJ \n(SHRI KAPIL MORESHWAR PATIL) \n \n(a)  Ministry of Panchayati Raj reviews the performance of Panchayati Raj Institutions (PRIs) \nthroughout the country from time to time through studies, review meetings, field visits, video \nconferencing, Information Technology applications, Common Review Missions (CRMs) etc. \nDetails in r espect of activities planned by the State of Odisha from Central Finance \nCommission Funds relating to management of rains and floods are as under:- \n \nAsset Name Number of \nActivities \nFY 2019-20 \nNumber of \nActivities \nFY 2020-21 \nNumber of \nActivities \nFY 2021-22 \nNumber of \nActivities \nFY 2022-23 \nTotal \nAmount \n(Rs. in \nlakh) \nDrainage  9403 13696 13530 5514 71771.16 \nMinor Irrigation \nStructures \n212 229 153 102 1484.75 \nReservoir 33 45 26 8 261.96 \nAmrit Sarovar 1 - - - 4.90 \nRain Water Harvest \nTank  \n396 759 445 173 4402.02 \nBarrage/Weir/Anicut 115 188 171 83 917.72 \nCheckdam 38 102 88 70 1075.89 \n\nDam 27 45 25 15 166.14 \nDrainage Channel - - 1 2 4.00 \nFY: Financial Year  (Source: Information furnished by the Panchayats of Odisha on e -\nGramswaraj Portal) \n \n(b) The number of women panchayat members  (Gram / Block / District) and women \nsarpanches in Odisha are 52,066 and 3,779 respectively, as reported by Government of Odisha. \n \n(c) The Ministry has launched eGramSwaraj, a Work Based Accounting Application for \nPRIs under the e-Panchayat Mission Mode Project on 24th April 2020. In Odisha, so far 6798 \nGram Panchayats (GPs) for 2021 -22 and 6766 GPs for 2022 -23 have prepared and uploaded \ntheir Gram Panchayat Development Plans (GPDPs). 6624 GPs have closed their year books for \nthe year 2021 -22 and 6484 GPs have closed month books for 2022 -23. Furthermore, under \n14th Finance Commission, 6714 GPs and 6382 GPs have carried out online transactions during \n2020- 21 and 2021-22 respectively. Similarly, 6734 GPs have carried online transactions under \nXV Finance Commission during 2021-22. \n \n(d) For the implementation of SVAMITVA Scheme , Memorandum of Understanding \nbetween Survey of India and Government of Odisha was signed on 5 th February, 2021. As on \n13th July, 2022, drone survey has been completed in 830 villages and 316 property cards have \nbeen prepared for residents of 12 villages of Rayagada district in the State.  \n \n*** \n \n \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S9_U_347": " \nGOVERNMENTOF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF FOOD PROCESSING INDUSTRIES \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 347 \nANSWERED ON 19TH JULY, 2022 \n \nINCREASING INCOME OF FARMERS BY FOOD PROCESSING \n \n \n†347. SHRI KUNWAR PUSHPENDRA SINGH CHANDEL: \n           \n       Will the Minister of FOOD PROCESSING INDUSTRIES be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government is taking initiative to increase the income of the farmers by helping them in \nthe food processing of their produce and for providing market for their products/final products; \n  \n(b) if so, the details thereof; and \n  \n(c) the details of the steps taken by the Government during the last five years to promote food processing \nin Bundelkhand? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR FOOD PROCESSING INDUSTRIES \n(SHRI PRAHLAD SINGH PATEL) \n \n(a) & (b): Ministry of Food Processing Industries (MoFPI) is implementing Central Sector Scheme–Pradhan \nMantri Kisan SAMPADA Yojana (PMKSY) for overall development of the food processing sector for \nincreasing processing capacity, reducing wa stage of agricultural produce, creating off -farm employment, \nensuring better returns to farmers and enhancing the export of the processed foods.  \n \nMoFPI is also implementing centrally sponsored \"PM Formalisation of Micro food processing \nEnterprises Scheme (PMFME)\". The scheme has adopted One District One Product (ODOP) approach to \nreap the benefit of scale in terms of procurement of inputs, availing common services and marketing of \nproducts. Under the scheme component, grant upto 50% to groups of FPOs/ SHGs/ Cooperatives or an SPV \nof micro food processing enterprises is available for Branding and Marketing Support. \n \nAnother Central Sector Scheme -Production Linked Incentive Scheme (PLIS) for Food Processing \nIndustry, for boosting manufacturing competitiveness , exports and creating global food manufacturing \nchampions and to support Indian brands of food products in the international markets, is also implementing \nby MoFPI. Under the scheme, Incentive is extended for the promotion of only Indian Brands abroad. \n \n(c): Apart from the implementation of schemes like Pradhan Mantri Kisan SAMPADA Yojana \n(PMKSY), PM Formalisation of Micro food processing Enterprises Scheme (PMFME), Production Linked \nIncentive Scheme (PLIS), the Government has taken several measures to support the growth of FPI sector in \nthe country including Bundelkhand e.g. classification of food & agro-based processing units and cold chain \ninfrastructure as agricultural activity for Priority Sector Lending (PSL), Special fund of National Bank for \nAgriculture and Rural Development (NABARD) for lending to food processing industries. In Bundelkhand \n13 district has been identified for One District One Product (ODOP) under PMFME and in PMKSY out of 4 \nsanctioned projects 2 projects have been completed. \n \n***** ",
    "ls|LS17_S9_U_348": " \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \n \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nLOK SABHA\n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.348\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 1\n9\nTH\n \nJULY\n, 2022\n \n \nCROP LOSS IN KUTTANAD AND PALAKKAD REGIONS\n \n \n \n348. SHRI \nKODIKUNNIL SURESH\n:\n \n        \nSHRI V.K. SREEKANDAN:\n \n  \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE \nAND\n \nFARMERS WELFARE \nकृ \u0000ष\n \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \nमं\u0000ी\n \nbe pleased to state:\n \n \n(a) whether the Government is aware of the large\n-\nscale losses repeatedly suffered by \npaddy cultivators of Kuttanad and Palakkad regions of Kerala due to unexpected torrential \nrains, floods and untimely rains in the summer season; \n \n(b) if so, whether the Kerala Government has sought action/help for paddy \ncultivators of \nthese regions; \n \n(c) if so, the details thereof; \n \n(d) whether it is also a fact that the affected farmers in the districts have been demanding \ncompensation for the loss suffered by them and demanded increase of the procurement \nprice for their\n \npaddy produce; \n \n(e) whether the Government has allocated any special compensation package for the \npaddy cultivators in the last three financial years or in the current year to subsume their \nlosses; and \n \n(f) if so, the details thereof?\n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF\n \nAGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \nकृ \u0000ष\n \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \nमं\u0000ी\n     \n \n(SHRI NARENDRA SINGH TOMAR)\n \n \n(a) t\no (f\n): \n \nCrop area affected due to hydro\n-\nmeteorological calamities/hazards during \n2021\n-\n22 in Kerala was 0.85 lakh\n \nhectares. However, Kerala has not reported crop area \naffected due to hydro\n-\nmeteorological calamities/hazards during 2020\n-\n21 and 2022\n-\n23 (as \non 15.07.2022). \n \n \n \n\n \n \n \nThe primary responsibility for disaster management rests with the State \nGovernments.  The State Governments provide financial relief to the affected people in \nthe wake of notified disasters from the State Disaster Response Fund (SDRF), already \nplaced at th\neir disposal. However, in the event of disaster of a severe nature, additional \nfinancial assistance is extended from the National Disaster Response Fund (NDRF), as \nper the laid down procedure, which includes an assessment based on the visit of an Inter\n-\nMin\nisterial Central Team (IMCT). \nUnder the extant guidelines of SDRF and NDRF, relief is \nprovided to the affected people for the damages caused due to notified natural calamities \nand not for compensation of loss suffered/claimed. \nDetails of \nFunds allocated\n/re\nleased\n \nto \nthe \nState Government of Kerala \nunder SDRF\n \nfrom 2020\n-\n21 to 2022\n-\n23\n,\n \nare as under:\n \n                                                                                                                      \n \n(Rs. in Crore)\n \nYear\n \nAllocation under SDRF\n \nCentral Share\n \nState Share\n \n2020\n-\n21\n \n251.20\n \n84.00\n \n2021\n-\n22\n \n251.20\n \n84.00\n \n2022\n-\n23\n \n \n264.00\n \n88.00\n \n \nThe State Government of Kerala has not submitted any memorandum seeking \nfinancial assistance \nfrom NDRF for \nhydro\n-\nmeteorological calamities/hazards\n \nduring 2020\n-\n21\n, 2021\n-\n22\n \nand 2022\n-\n23\n \n(so far).\n \n \n*******\n \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S9_U_349": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF INFORMATION AND BROADCASTING \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.349 \n(TO BE ANSWERED ON 19.07.2022) \n \nCLASSICAL MUSIC PROGRAMMES \n349. ADV. A.M. ARIFF: \n         Will the Minister of INFORMATION AND BROADCASTING be \npleased to state: \n(a) whether the Government has taken any decision to \nbroadcast Hindi programmes at prime time slots through \nFrequency Modulation (FM) stations of All India Radio (AIR) \nincluding th ose in Kerala by replacing the popular \nprogrammes in the local languages, if so, the details thereof \nand if not, the reasons therefor; \n(b) whether the Government is aware that classical music \nprogrammes broadcasted by the Thiruvananthapuram Station \nof AIR on a  daily basis in the morning have been abruptly \nstopped and if so, the reason therefor; \n(c) whether the Government will take immediate steps for \nrestoring the classical music programmes broadcasted by \nthe Thiruvananthapuram Station of AIR on a daily basis in \nthe morning; \n(d) if so, the details thereof and if not the reason thereof; and \n(e) the details of funds allocated for AIR stations in Kerala from \nFY 2018-19 to FY 2021-22? \n\nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF INFORMATION AND BROADCASTING ; AND \nMINISTER OF YOUTH AFFAIRS & SPORTS  (SHRI ANURAG SINGH \nTHAKUR) \n \n(a) Prasar  Bharati always strives to improve its program me by \ncontinuous evaluation and modification of the programme \nschedule as per audience feedback in all FM stations of All India \nRadio. The programme schedule is a mix of bo th Hindi and local \nlanguage in most of the FM channels out of which majority of the \ntime is given to local language. FM Aakashvani  Ananthapuri \nVividh Bharati Malayalam of AIR Thiruvananthapuram is relaying \nonly 3 hours of entertainment, out of 17:30 h ours of original \nprogramming. Similarly, Real FM Kozhikode (Calicut) airs only \nfour hours of national Vividh  Bharati slots out of its 17:30 hours \nof transmission.  Except these two FM channels, all other AIR \nchannels in Kerala are broadcasting only local programmes. \n(b) The schedule of classical music programme on AIR \nThiruvananthapuram is being broadcast twice a week . The local \nstation is at liberty to broadcast its own recorded or live content \nas per its Fixed Point chart or on popular demand.  \n(c) & (d) Considering the listener ’s requirements, the programme \ncomposition is reviewed once in six months. \n(e) Rs.23.10 Crore was allocated for AIR Stations in Kerala during \nthe Financial Years 2018-19 to 2021-22. \n******* ",
    "ls|LS17_S9_U_350": " \n GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 350 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 19TH JULY, 2022/ASHADHA 28, 1944 (SAKA) \n \nNATIONAL REGISTER OF CITIZENS \n \n350.  SHRI ABDUL KHALEQUE: \n \nWill the Minister of HOME AFFAIRS be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether the Government is aware of the report  that the Supreme Court \nmonitored final NRC list published on 31.08.2019 is provisional; \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n \n(c) whether the Government considers this list to be  the final one, if so, the \ndetails thereof and if not, the reasons therefor; and \n \n(d) the reasons for not starting the process of appeal and rejection slip? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n(SHRI NITYANAND RAI) \n(a) to (d):  On  the  di rections  of  the  Hon’ble  Supreme  Court,  the  \nsupplementary  list  of inclusions  and  exclusions  for National Register of \nCitizens (NRC), Assam have  been  published  on  31st  August,  2019. The \nHon’ble Supreme  Court  has  directed  the  State  Coordinator of NRC, Assam  \nto  enact an appropriate  security  regime  on  lines  similar  to  the  security  \nregime  provided  for Aadhaar  data  and  only  thereafter  the  list  of \ninclusions  and  exclusions  shall  be made  available  to  the  State  \nGovernment,  Central  Government  and  Registrar General of India.  Any \nperson who is not satisfied with the outcome of the decisions of the claims \nand  objections  under  supplementary  list  as  per  Clause 7 of  the Schedule  \n\n--2-- \nL.S.US.Q.NO. 350 FOR 19.07.2022 \n \nappended to the Citizenship Rules, 2003 may prefer an appeal before the \ndesignated Foreigners Tribunals within a period of 120  days from the date of \nsuch order. \n \n***** \n   \n  \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S9_U_351": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF INFORMATION AND BROADCASTING \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 351 \n(TO BE ANSWERED ON 19.07.2022) \n \nCOMMUNITY RADIO STATIONS \n \n351. SHRI GYANESHWAR PATIL: \n \nWill the Minister of INFORMATION & BROADCASTING be pleased to state: \n \na) whether any innovative inventions or studies have been done by the Government so that the \ninformation about the welfare schemes of the Government reaches to the people at the \nPanchayat level and also tribal areas regularly and if so, the details thereof; \nb) whether the Government has any plan to link public administration offices with e-Governance \nfor better transparency and productivity across the country; \nc) whether the Government proposes to streamline the process of setting up community radio \nstations and en courage it in rural and urban areas so that information can be easily \naccessed in each area; and \nd) if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF INFORMATION AND BROADCASTING; AND MINISTER OF YOUTH \nAFFAIRS AND SPORTS \n(SHRI ANURAG SINGH THAKUR) \n \n(a) Press Information Bureau (PIB), as part of the central sector scheme \n‘Dissemination of Information & Development Communication (DCID)’, implements the \nsub scheme ‘Media Outreach Programme’, under which VARTALAP is one of the activity, \nwherein media conclav es are  conducted at district level and rural media at panchayat \nlevel is invited for one -day interactive session s. It has been designed to inform and \nempower potential beneficiaries at village level of the development programmes and \n\n \nschemes of the Government of India  and to participate in and avail of the benefits of \nthese schemes through rural media. Further, the press releases and PM's speeches and \ntweets are translated and disseminated to media in regional languages. \nBesides the above, t he Central Bureau of Communication (CBC, e rstwhile BOC) \ncarries out awareness campaign on Government policies and programmes on behalf of \nMinistries/Departments in rural and tribal areas. \n \n(b) The Government of India, as a part of e -Governance, has implemented e -Office \nwith the objective of improving the Government functioning by inculcating more efficient, \neffective, transparent office procedures , thus increasing the accountability and \nresponsibility in the Inter-Government and Intra-Government transactions. \n \n(c) and (d)  The Government of India has taken several measures for streamlining the \nprocess of setting up Community Radio Stations (CRS) and encouraging it in rural and \nurban areas. The Gov ernment has revamped its Broadcastseva Portal, for online \nsubmission of applications for grant of permissions for setting up of Community Radio \nStations. Further, the Government , from time to time,  organizes Community Radio  \nawareness workshops for increasing awareness regarding setting up of Community \nRadio Stations. During the financial year 2021-22, a total of nine webinars were organized \nby the Ministry of Information and Broadcasting for increasing awareness amongst the \npotential organizations, encouraging them to setup Community Radio Stations in various \nparts of the country. \n**** ",
    "ls|LS17_S9_U_352": "OIH \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURAL RESEARCH & EDUCATION \n \n \nLOK SABHA  \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 352 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 19th July, 2022 \n \n \nOPENING AGRICULTURAL UNIVERSITIES \n \n \n352. SHRI VIJAY KUMAR DUBEY: \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE               \nd`f\"k vkSj fdlku dY;k.k ea=h  be pleased to state: \n(a) whether the Government proposes to open Agricultural Universities in each zone \nas an incentive to  farmers to connect them with modern farming system across the \ncountry, particularly in an agriculture dominated State like Uttar Pradesh; \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n(c) the number of districts of Uttar Pradesh which have been identified for \nimplementation of the said scheme; and \n(d) the time by which the said scheme is likely to be implemented? \n \n \nANSWER \n \n \n THE MINISTER OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE      \nd`f\"k vkSj fdlku dY;k.k ea=h        (SHRI NARENDRA SINGH TOMAR) \n \n(a):  Agriculture including Agricultural Education being the state subject. Opening of \nAgricultural Universities is primarily the responsibility of concerned State Government. \nHowever, there is already a Central Agricultural University in Jhansi, Uttar Pradesh. At \npresent, there is no proposal to open any additional Central Agricultural University in \nthe state of Uttar Pradesh. \n \n(b) to (d): Question does not arise. \n***** \n \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S9_U_353": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 353 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 19TH JULY, 2022/ ASHADHA 28, 1944 (SAKA) \n \nSTRENGTHENING OF FSLs. \n \n353.  SHRI N.K. PREMACHANDRAN: \n \nWill the Minister of HOME AFFAIRS be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether the Government has any proposal to strengthen Forensic \nScience Laboratories (FSLs) in the country and if so, the details thereof; \n \n(b) whether the Government is aware that the problem of lack of skilled \nand experienced human resources is faced by the Forensic Analysis; \n \n(c) if so, the action taken by the Government in this regard; \n \n(d) whether the Government proposes to establish campus of National \nForensic Science University in all States and if so, the details thereof; \n \n(e) whether the Government proposes to consider the application for \nestablishing campus of National Forensic Science University at Kollam in \nthe vacant land available with Central Government Public Undertaking and \nif so, the details thereof; and \n \n(f) whether the Government proposes to establish regional centre forensic \nlab at Kollam in Kerala and if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n(SHRI AJAY KUMAR MISHRA) \n(a)  The Ministry of Home Affairs has taken several steps for \nstrengthening of Forensic Science Laboratories in the country, and \ninclude, inter-alia: \n \n\n-2- \nL.S.US.Q.NO. 353 FOR 19.07.2022 \n(i)  Modernization of Central Forensic Sciences Laboratories at Bhopal, \nGuwahati, Pune and Kolkata has been undertaken at a total cost of \nRs.253.27 crore. The modern laboratories at Bhopal, Guwahati and Pune \nhave already commenced functioning.  \n(ii) Upgrading of machinery & equipment in the Central Forensic \nSciences Laboratories, including in the new disciplines of Forensics in \nNarcotic Drugs & Psychotropic Substances, Digital Forensics, DNA \nForensic analysis, Forensic Psychology. Procurement of 269 equipment \nhave already been completed at a total cost of Rs.55.62 Crore. \n(iii) Setting-up a State-of-the-art DNA Analysis and Research & \nDevelopment facility at the Central Forensic Sciences Laboratory at \nChandigarh \n(iv) Setting up a National Cyber Forensic Science Laboratory at the \nCentral Forensic Sciences Laboratory, Hyderabad to investigate important \ncases of digital fraud / cyber forensics.  \n(v) Operationalizing an e-Forensics IT platform, which connects 117 \nforensic science laboratories (Central and State) in the country.  \n \n \n \n\n-3- \nL.S.US.Q.NO. 353 FOR 19.07.2022 \n(vi) Approval of assistance to States/ Union Territories for strengthening \nDNA analysis, Cyber-forensics and related facilities in States/Union \nTerritories. 23 States/ Union Territories have already availed Rs.203.45 \ncrore as assistance.  \n(vii) Approval of a Scheme for Modernization of Forensic Capacities for \nassisting States/ Union Territories to develop high quality forensic science \nfacilities in the States/UTs to facilitate timely investigation through \nmodernization of machinery and equipment after a gap-analysis, and \nfacilitating availability of trained manpower in these laboratories. \n(viii) The Directorate of Forensic Science Services under the Ministry of \nHome Affairs, to ensure quality and standardization in forensic \nexamination in the Forensic Science Laboratories in the country, has \nissued Quality Manuals for accreditation of laboratories as per NABL \nstandards (ISO 17025); Working Procedure Manuals in various disciplines \nof Forensic Sciences; Guidelines for collection, preservation & \ntransportation of forensic evidence in sexual assault cases for \nInvestigation Officers and medical Officers; a Standard list of Equipment \nfor establishing/upgrading of Forensic Sciences Laboratories; and \nStandard Operating Procedures for Crime Scene Investigation.  \n \n \n\n-4- \nL.S.US.Q.NO. 353 FOR 19.07.2022 \n(b) and (c):Taking into account the shortage of skilled and qualified \nmanpower in the field of forensic sciences in our country, the Government \nhas established the National Forensic Sciences University in the year 2020 \nas an Institution of National Importance through an Act of Parliament. The \nNational Forensic Sciences University has also set up a Centre of \nExcellence in the field of Narcotic Drugs & Psychotropic Substances in \ncollaboration with the Narcotics Control Bureau for research and analysis.  \n(d):  In order to enhance the outreach, the National Forensic Sciences \nUniversity has requested States/ Union Territories to identify their \ncolleges/Institutions for affiliation with the University to undertake \nforensic sciences courses. At present, apart from the campuses at \nGandhinagar and Delhi, the University has commenced two new campuses \nin Goa and Tripura. Opening of a new campus by the University is an \nongoing process and is a function of feasibility and demand.  \n(e):  There is no such proposal under consideration at present.  \n(f): Setting up of new Regional forensic science facility is a State subject \nand is dependent on the specific requirement of implementation of law by \nthe States/ Union Territories.  \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S9_U_354": "O.I.H.\n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nLOK\n \nSABHA\n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO\n. \n354\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON \n19\nTH\nJUL\nY, 2022\n \n \nBAN ON WHEAT EXPORT\n \n \n354\n.\n \nSHRI \nKAUSHALENDRA KUMAR:\n \nSHRI RAJIV \nRANJAN SINGH ALIAS LALAN SINGH\n:\n \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \nकृ\u0000ष और \u0000कसान क\u0000याण \nमं\u0000ी\n \nbe pleased to state:\n \n \n(\na\n)\n \nwhether the Government proposes to provide any assistance/compensation on \nthe losses suffered by farmers due to ban on \nthe export of wheat\n;\n \n \n(\nb\n)\n \nwhether the Government is fully aware of the news given by the government of \nTurkey about a particular type of virus in Indian wheat; and\n \n \n(\nc\n)\n \nif so, the steps being taken by the Government to deal with such attempt to \ndefame the country\n?\n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nTHE \nMINISTER OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \nकृ\u0000ष और \u0000कसान क\u0000याण मं\u0000ी\n \n \n(SHRI NARENDRA SINGH TOMAR)\n \n \n(a):\n \nThere are no losses suffered by farmers due to ban on the export of wheat\n. \nExport of wheat\n \nis done in bulk containerized cargo by exporters. Moreover, domestic \nprices of wheat are ruling above MSP (Minimum Support Price) even after ban on the \nexport of wheat\n.\n \n \n(b):\n \nSome newspapers reported that Indian wheat consignment was rejected due to \nRubel\nla virus. This virus is found in human beings and it is not associated with wheat or \nany other plant products.\n \nNational Plant Protection Organisation (NPPO) of Turkey officially \ninformed that \nIndian wheat con\nsignment was rejected due to contamination of “Karnal Bunt” of wheat \n\npathogen (\nTilletiaindica\n). However, the wheat consignment was inspected as per \nstandard operating procedures for export of wheat and was found to be free from Karnal \nBunt\n. \nT\nhe same consignment was \nsent to \nIsrael \nwhich was accepted by the Israeli \nGovernment\n.\n \n \n(c):\n \nStrict quarantine measures are in place\n \nwhen exporting consignment outside \nIndia. The consignments are inspected by Plant Quarantine inspectors, laboratory \ntested an\nd exported adhering to the phytosanitary requirements of other countries\n.\n \n*****\n \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S9_U_355": " \n GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 355 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 19TH JULY, 2022/ASHADHA 28, 1944 (SAKA) \n \nFIRE IN GHAZIPUR LANDFILL  \n \n355. SHRI GAURAV GOGOI: \n \nWill the Minister of HOME AFFAIRS be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) the total number of fire cases reported from the  Ghazipur landfill during \nthe last five years; \n \n(b) whether the Government has conducted any  study for potential causes of \nthese fires, if so, details thereof and if not reasons therefor; \n \n(c) whether the Government has issued any  guidelines to manage waste in \nlandfills and if so, the details thereof; and \n \n(d) whether the Government is aware that the landfill  poses major health \nhazards to drivers unloading waste  at the site and al l the people residing \nnear it and if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n(SHRI NITYANAND RAI) \n(a) to (d) :  As per the input provided by the Municipal Corporation of Delhi \n(MCD), the year -wise details of fire  incidents at Sanitary Landfill Site (SLF), \nGhazipur during last five years are as under: \nFinancial Year Total No. of Fire incidents \n2017-18 31 \n2018-19 18 \n2019-20 06 \n2020-21 06 \n2021-22 03 \n \n\n--2-- \nL.S.US.Q.NO. 355 FOR 19.07.2022 \n \nThe MCD has informed that the National Environment Eng ineering \nResearch Institute (NEE RI) has conducted a study on potential causes of \nfires at SLF. The study inter -alia analyzed the risk of potential ignition of \nvarious types of waste based on their age with seasonal variations.    \n  For regulating the solid waste management, the Ministry of \nEnvironment and Forests has framed the Solid Waste Management Rules, \n2016. A well defined Standard Operating Procedure (SOP) is also in place. \nThe MCD follows these provisions scrupulously  for Solid Waste Management, \nfor obviating any probable health hazards.  \n \n***** \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S9_U_356": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HEAVY INDUSTRIES  \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 356 \nANSWERED ON 19.07.2022 \n \nCHARGING STATIONS FOR ELECTRIC VEHICLES \n \n356. SHRIMATI KAVITHA MALOTHU:  \nDR. G. RANJITH REDDY:  \nSHRI VENKATESH NETHA BORLAKUNTA:  \n \nWill the Minister of HEAVY INDUSTRIES भारी उद्योग मंत्री be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the total amount sanctioned, approved, released and spent on charging stations for \nelectric vehicles under Phase -I and II of Faster Adoption and Manufacturing of Hybrid and \nElectric Vehicles in India (FAME- India);  \n \n(b) the details of charging stations set up in cities of Telangana under Phase -I and II of \nthis scheme, district-wise and city-wise;  \n \n(c) the details of charging stations set up on Expressways of Telangana under Phase -I and \nII;  \n \n(d) the details of charging stations set up on National Highways under Phase-I and II;  \n \n(e) the details of charging stations set up at petroleum retail outlets in Telangana, district -\nwise;  \n \n(f) whether the Government thinks that the above charging stations are sufficient; and  \n \n(g) if not, the steps taken by the Government to set up more and more charging stations in \ncities, on National Highways, expressways and at petroleum outlets in Telang ana in the \ncoming two-three years? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR HEAVY INDUSTRIES \n(SHRI KRISHAN PAL GURJAR) \n \n(a) to (e):  Under FAME India Scheme Phase -I, the Ministry of Heavy Industries had \nsanctioned 520 Charging Stations/ Infrastructure for Rs. 43 Crore (approx.) . Out of 520 EV \ncharging Station, 479 EV Charging stations are commissioned and under operation as on 1 st \nJuly, 2022.  Under FAME India Scheme Phase -II, Ministry of Heavy Industries has \nsanctioned 2877 EV Charging S tations (Estimated amount Rs. 500 Cr.) in 68 cities across 25 \nstates/UTs and out of these EV Charging Stations Letter of Awards has been issued for 1822 \nEV charging stations.  Out of these 1822 EV Charging Stations 50 EV Charging Stations have \nbeen installed and commissioned as on 14th July, 2022.  \n  \n \n \n \n\n-2- \n \n \nFurther, 1576 charging stations across 9  Expressways and 16 Highways under Phase II of \nFAME India Scheme has also been  sanctioned by the Ministry of Heavy Industries. The details of \ncharging stations State/UTs-wise is at ANNEXURE-I. \n \n(f) & (g): Sir, following initiatives have also been taken up by the Government for \nimproving the charging infrastructure required for electr ic vehicles in the country including \nthe State of Telangana- \n(i) Ministry of Power ( MoP) has released a notification on charging infrastructure standards \npermitting private charging at residences and offices.  \n(ii) Ministry of Housing & Urban Affairs (MoHUA) amended the Model Building Byelaws \n2016 to establish charging stations and infrastructure in private and commercial buildings. \n*********  \n \n \n \n \n \n  \n\n-3- \nANNEXURE-I \n1. Under Phase -I of FAME India Scheme  – Ministry of Heavy Industries has sanctioned  520 \nEV Charging Stations out of which 479 charging stations have been installed as on 01st July, 2022 \nas follows: \n \nState/ UT Charging Stations Highway Charging Stations \nChandigarh 48 Delhi -Chandigarh 24 \nDelhi 94 Mum-Pune 17 \nRajasthan 49 Delhi- Jaipur- Agra 31 \nKarnataka 65 Jaipur-Delhi Highway 9 \nJharkhand 30   \nUttar Pradesh 16   \nGoa 30   \nHyderabad (Telangana) 57   \nHimachal Pradesh 9   \nTotal 398  81 \n \n2.  Under Phase-II of FAME India Scheme: \n➢ Ministry of Heavy Industries has sanctioned 2877 EV Charging Stations in 68 cities \nacross 25 states/UTs as on 15th July, 2022. \nState No. of EV chargers sanctioned \nMaharashtra 317 \nAndhra Pradesh 266 \nTamil Nadu 281 \nGujarat 278 \nUttar Pradesh 207 \nRajasthan 205 \nKarnataka 172 \nMadhya Pradesh 235 \nWest Bengal 141 \nTelangana (Hyderabad- 118, Warangal- 10, \nKarimnagar- 10) 138 \nKerala 211 \nDelhi 72 \nChandigarh 70 \nHaryana 50 \nMeghalaya 40 \nBihar 37 \nSikkim 29 \nJammu & Kashmir 25 \nChhattisgarh 25 \nAssam 20 \nOdisha 18 \nUttarakhand 10 \nPuducherry 10 \nAndaman and Nicobar (Port Blair) 10 \n\nHimachal Pradesh 10 \nTotal 2877 \n \n➢ MHI has sanctioned 1576 EV Charging Stations across 9 Expressways & 16 \nHighways. Details are as specified below: \nSl. No Expressways EV Charging Stations Sanctioned \n1  Mumbai - Pune  10 \n2  Ahmadabad - Vadodara  10 \n3  Delhi Agra Yamuna  20 \n4  Bengaluru Mysore  14 \n5  Bangaluru-Chennai  30 \n6  Surat-Mumbai  30 \n7  Agra-Lucknow  40 \n8  Eastern Peripheral (A)  14 \n9  Hyderabad ORR  16 \nSl. No Highways EV Charging Stations Sanctioned \n1  Delhi - Srinagar 80 \n2  Delhi – Kolkata  160 \n3  Agra - Nagpur 80 \n4  Meerut to Gangotri Dham 44 \n5  Mumbai - Delhi  124 \n6  Mumbai-Panaji  60 \n7  Mumbai - Nagpur  70 \n8  Mumbai - Bengaluru 100 \n9  Kolkata - Bhubaneswar 44 \n10  Kolkata - Nagpur  120 \n11  Kolkata - Gangtok   76 \n12  Chennai-Bhubaneswar 120 \n13  Chennai - Trivendram  74 \n14  Chennai-Ballary  62 \n15  Chennai - Nagpur  114 \n16  Mangaldai - Wakro  64 \n1576 \n3.0 State/UT No. of Petroleum Retail Outlets (Ros) where EV Charging facility is \navailable as on 1.1.2022 \nState/UT No of RO's where EV Charging Facility available \nAndhra Pradesh 65 \nArunachal Pradesh 4 \nAssam 19 \nBihar 26 \nChandigarh 4 \nChhattisgarh 51 \nDelhi 66 \nGoa 17 \nGujarat 87 \nHaryana 114 \nHimachal Pradesh 13 \nJharkhand 22 \n            J&K 3 \n\nKarnataka 100 \nKerala 39 \nLeh 2 \n Madhya Pradesh 167 \n        Maharastra 88 \nManipur 1 \nMeghalaya 3 \nNagaland 2 \nOdisha 26 \nPondicherry 2 \nPunjab 41 \nRajasthan 174 \nTamil Nadu 76 \nTelangana 112 \nTripura 3 \nUttar Pradesh 128 \nUttarakhand 10 \nWest Bengal 71 \nGrand Total 1536 \n \n***** \n \n \n \n \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S9_U_357": "  \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \nLOK  SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 357 \n  ANSWERED ON 19/07/2022 \n \nUNEMPLOYMENT ALLOWANCE UNDER MGNREGS \n \n357.  SHRI COSME FRANCISCO CAITANO SARDINHA: \n \nWill the Minister of RURAL DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  the number of households that have been offered guaranteed employment during the \nlast two financial years (2020 -21) and (2021 -2022) under the Mahatma Gandhi \nNational Rural Employment Guarantee Scheme (MGNREGS); \n(b)  whether the beneficiary is eligible for unemployment allowances under MGNREGS \nin case of non-offering of work; and \n(c)  if so, the details of the total number of such beneficiaries and total unemployment \nallowances given during the said period, State-wise? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT  \n(SADHVI NIRANJAN JYOTI) \n \n(a)     The details of households who have been offered employment under Mahatma Gandhi \nNational Rural Employment Guarantee Scheme (Mahatma Gandhi NREGS) in last two \nfinancial years 2020-21 and 2021-22 is given below: \n  \n Financial Year  2020-21 2021-22 \nHouseholds who have been offered employment  \n(figure in crore) \n8.54 8.02 \n  \n(b)   As per Section 7(1) of Mahatma Gandhi National Rural Employment Guarantee Act \n(Mahatma Gandhi NREGA), “If an applicant for employment under the Scheme is not \nprovided such employment within fifteen days of receipt of his application seeking \nemployment or from the date on which the employment has been sought in the case of an \nadvance application, whichever is later, he shall be entitled to a daily unemployment \nallowance in accordance with this section.” \n  \n(c)  State/UT-wise details of unemployment allowance under  Mahatma Gandhi NREGS in \nthe financial year 2020-21 are given at Annexure-I.  \nState/UT-wise details of unemployment allowance under  Mahatma Gandhi NREGS in the \nfinancial 