    "ls|LS17_S9_U_388": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF COOPERATION \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO 388 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 19th JULY 2022 \n \nFUNCTIONING OF MULTI-STATES COOPERATIVE SOCIETIES \n \n388: SHRI L.S. TEJASVI SURYA: \nDR. UMESH G. JADHAV: \nSHRI SHYAM SINGH YADAV: \nSHRI PRATHAP SIMHA: \nSHRI KUMBAKUDI SUDHAKARAN: \nSHRI SHA.BRA.DR.JAI SIDDESHWAR SHIVACHARYA MAHASWAMIJI: \nPROF. SOUGATA RAY: \nSHRI D.M. KATHIR ANAND: \nSHRI SANGANNA AMARAPPA: \nSHRI S. MUNISWAMY: \nSHRI GNANATHIRAVIAM S \n \nWill the Minister of Co-operation (सहकारिता मंत्री) be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) the Multi -State Cooperative Societies registered and operational in the country, \nState/UT-wise including in Jaunpur district in Uttar Pradesh alongwith year of formation;  \n(b) the steps taken by the Government to have a common headquarter for several Multi -\nState, nationwide cooperative societies which deal with agriculture, textiles, handicrafts, \nrealtors and other multi varied sectors with their branches in various States;  \n(c) whether these nation -wide cooperative societies are allowed to operate their financial \naccounts and administrative works from any where in the country and if so, the details \nthereof;  \n(d) the number of new Multi -State co -operative societies that were sanctioned after the \nformation of Ministry of Cooperation, State-wise;  \n(e) whether the Government has noticed that a large number of societies registered in \nrecord are not functioning, and if so, the steps being taken to deregister such co-opertive \nsocieties; and  \n(f) the steps being taken/intended to be taken by the Government for ensuring vigilance \nover the registered cooperative societies? \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF COOPERATION सहकारिता मंत्री (Shri Amit Shah) \n \n(a) The State/UT-wise list of Multi-State Cooperative Societies registered  is attached at \nAnnexure-I, out of which eighty-one societies are under liquidation and year-wise (2001 to \n2021) list of Multi-State Cooperative Societies registered  is attached at Annexure -II. One \nsociety, namely, Gramin Kisan Vikas Seva Multipurpose Cooperative  Society Ltd  was \nregistered in Jaunpur District of Uttar Pradesh in 2013.  \n \n(b) and (c) As per the provisions  of the Multi State Cooperative Societies Act, 2002 and \nrules made thereunder, every multi-State cooperative society shall have one principal place \nof business which shall be the registered office of the society . The registered address of a \nsociety can be anywhere in the country as specified in its bye-laws. \n \n(d) The list of new Multi-State co-operative societies that were registered after the formation \nof Ministry of Cooperation, State-wise is placed at Annexure-III.  \n \n(e) As per record, eighty -one multi -state Cooperative Societies are under liquidation in \nwhich process of winding up have been initiated. \n \n(f) As and when instances of irregularities in any Multi-State co-operative society (MSCS) \ncome to notice, action is taken under the relevant provisions of Multi State Cooperative \nSocieties Act, 2002 and rules made thereunder. Further, powers of inspection of the MSCS \nhave been delegated to the Registrar of Cooperative Societies of all States/ UTs under \nsection 108 of MSCS Act 2002. \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \n \nAnnexure-I \nDetail of registered societies and societies under liquidation in different States/ UTs in \nthe country under Multi State Co-operative Societies as on 30 June 2022 \n \nSNO  State/UT Name Societies registered Societies Under \nLiquidation \n1 ANDHRA PRADESH 22 4 \n2 ARUNACHAL PRADESH 1 - \n3 ASSAM 6 - \n4 BIHAR 19 - \n5 CHANDIGARH 1 1 \n6 CHHATTISGARH 8 - \n7 DADRA AND NAGAR HAVELI 1 - \n8 GOA 1 1 \n9 GUJARAT 42 4 \n10 HARYANA 17 - \n11 HIMACHAL PRADESH 1 - \n12 JAMMU AND KASHMIR 2 - \n13 JHARKHAND 8 1 \n14 KARNATAKA 29 - \n15 KERALA 25 - \n16 MADHYA PRADESH 29 - \n17 MAHARASHTRA 655 15 \n18 MANIPUR 3 - \n19 NAGALAND 1 - \n20 NEW DELHI 159 10 \n21 ODISHA 19 11 \n22 PONDICHERRY 5 - \n23 PUNJAB 23 1 \n24 RAJASTHAN 72 15 \n25 SIKKIM 1 - \n26 TAMIL NADU 123 4 \n27 TELANGANA 8 - \n28 UTTAR PRADESH 154 9 \n29 UTTARAKHAND 4 - \n30 WEST BENGAL 69 5 \n  Total 1508 81 \n \n\nAnnexure-II \nYear-wise detail of registered societies  in the country under Multi State Co -operative \nSocieties from 2001 to 2021: \n \nSNO  Year Societies registered \n1 2001 20 \n2 2002 15 \n3 2003 16 \n4 2004 35 \n5 2005 28 \n6 2006 15 \n7 2007 16 \n8 2008 31 \n9 2009 33 \n10 2010 54 \n11 2011 95 \n12 2012 247 \n13 2013 194 \n14 2014 250 \n15 2015 83 \n16 2016 19 \n17 2017 7 \n18 2018 8 \n19 2019 9 \n20 2020 10 \n21 2021 13 \n \n \n \n\nAnnexure-III \nList of registered societies registered after the creation of New Cooperation Ministry \nunder Multi State Co-operative Societies as on 30 June 2022 \n \nSNO  State Name Societies \n1 ANDHRA PRADESH 1 \n2 ARUNACHAL PRADESH - \n3 ASSAM - \n4 BIHAR - \n5 CHANDIGARH - \n6 CHHATTISGARH - \n7 DADRA AND NAGAR HAVELI - \n8 GOA - \n9 GUJARAT 3 \n10 HARYANA 1 \n11 HIMACHAL PRADESH - \n12 JAMMU AND KASHMIR 1 \n13 JHARKHAND - \n14 KARNATAKA - \n15 KERALA 4 \n16 MADHYA PRADESH 1 \n17 MAHARASHTRA 17 \n18 MANIPUR 2 \n19 NAGALAND - \n20 NEW DELHI 2 \n21 ODISHA - \n22 PONDICHERRY - \n23 PUNJAB - \n24 RAJASTHAN - \n25 SIKKIM - \n26 TAMIL NADU 1 \n27 TELANGANA 1 \n28 UTTAR PRADESH 5 \n29 UTTARAKHAND -- \n30 WEST BENGAL - \n  Total 39 \n \n \n \n \n \n \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S9_U_389": "O.I.H \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF FISHERIES, ANIMAL HUSBANDRY AND DAIRYING \nDEPARTMENT OF ANIMAL HUSBANDRY AND DAIRYING \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.389 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 19TH JULY, 2022 \n \n \nPROMOTION OF COOPERATIVE SOCIETIES FOR MILK PRODUCTION \n \n389. SHRI CHANDAN SINGH: \nSHRI DILESHWAR KAMAIT: \nSHRI JYOTIRMAY SINGH MAHATO: \nMS. LOCKET CHATTERJEE: \n \nWill the Minister of FISHERIES, ANIMAL HUSBANDRY AND DAIRYING क ृषि और कक सान क ल्याण मंत्री \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government has taken any initiative to promote cooperative societies for production of milk, \nother milk products, animal food etc. in all the States on the lines of Gujarat to ensure additional income for \nthe farmers; \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof; and \n \n(c) if not, the reasons therefor? \nANSWER \nTHE HON’BLE MINISTER OF FISHERIES, ANIMAL HUSBANDRY AND DAIRYING \n(SHRI PARSHOTTAM RUPALA) \n \n(a) to (c) The Department of Animal Husbandry & Dairying, Government of India is implementing following \nschemes primarily focusing on development of cooperative dairy sector including dairy co-operative societies: \n(i) National Programme for Dairy Development (NPDD): \n \nThe Department is implementing NPDD scheme across the country since Feb -2014 with an objective of \ncreating/strengthening of infrastructure for production of quality milk, procurement, processing and marketing \nof milk & milk products by dairy cooperatives, milk producer companies across the country . The scheme has \nbeen restructured/ realigned in July 2021 for implementation during 2021-22 to 2025-26. \n  \n(ii) Dairy Processing and Infrastructure Development Fund (DIDF): \n \nDIDF was launched in December 2017 with the objective of creation/ strengthening of Milk Processing, Value \naddition & chilling facilities for Dairy Co -operative, Multi State Dairy Coope ratives, Milk Producer \n\nCompanies (MPC), National Dairy Development Board ’s (NDDBs) subsidiaries, Self Help Groups ( SHGs) \nand Farmer Producer Organisations (FPOs) registered under State Cooperative and Companies Act with total \noutlay of Rs. 11,184 Crore (loan component of Rs. 8,004 Crore). DIDF is to be implemented from 2018-19 to \n2022-23 and repayment period upto 2030-31 with spill over to first quarter of the FY 2031-32.  \n \n(iii) Supporting Dairy Cooperatives and Farmer Producer Organizations engaged in dairy activities \n(SDCFPO): \n \nThe central sector scheme Supporting Dairy Cooperatives and Farmer Producer Organizations engaged in dairy \nactivities (SDCFPO) is implemented as a part of umbrella Scheme “Infrastructure Development Fund ” from \n2021-22 to 2025-26 with an outlay of Rs 500 Cr. This Scheme was launched during 2017 -18. The scheme is \nbeing implemented through National Dairy Development Board (NDDB). Main objective of the scheme is to \nassist the cooperative societies and farmer producer organizations engaged in dairy activities by providing soft \nworking capital loan to tide over the crisis on account of severely adverse market conditions or natural \ncalamities. Due to Economic  Impact of Covid -19 on dairy sector, Ministry of Fisherie s, Animal Husbandry \nand Dairying  has introduced a new component  “interest subvention on Working capital loans for Dairy \nsector” as one of the component  with an outlay of Rs 203 Cr for 2020 -21. Thus the actual implementation of \nscheme started during 2020-21. The other component namely “working Capital loan” was kept in suspension \nsince 2020-21. \n \n(iv) Rashtriya Gokul Mission (RGM): \n \nRGM scheme is being implemented with the objectives of enhancing productivity of bovines and increasing \nmilk production in sustainable manner using advanced technologies as well as to promote indigenous cattle & \nbuffaloes rearing and its conservation in a scientific and holistic manner.  Dairy cooperatives are also eligible \nto get assistance under the scheme. \n \n(v) National Livestock Mission (NLM): \n \nThe Government of India is implementing realigned National Livestock Mission (NLM) from FY 2021-22 for \nimproving fodder availability in the country. The sub-mission on Feed and Fodder Development of this scheme \naim towards strengthening of fodder seed chain to improve availability of certified fodder seed required for \nfodder production. National Dairy Development Board (NDDB) is facilitating dairy cooperatives to take up \nthe fodder seed production in a big way under the aegis of this scheme.  \n \n***** ",
    "ls|LS17_S9_U_390": "  \nO.I.H \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \nLOK  SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 390 \n  ANSWERED ON 19/07/2022 \n \n \nCONSTRUCTION OF HOUSES UNDER PMAY-G \n \n390.  SHRI SANJAY JADHAV: \nSHRI RAMDAS C. TADAS: \nSHRI KRUPAL BALAJI TUMANE: \nSHRI DIBYENDU ADHIKARI: \n \nWill the Minister of RURAL DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  the total number of houses constructed under  Pradhan Mantri Awas Yojana -Gramin \n(PMAY-G) during the last three years and the current year and the number of houses \nlikely to be constructed, State and district -wise, particularly in Wardha and Amravati \ndistricts of Maharashtra; \n(b)  the number of houses sanctioned/allotted in  Parbhani, Dadra and Nagar Haveli, \nDaman and Diu  thereunder during the last th ree years and the current  status thereof \nalong with the details of the funds allocated  and budget sanctioned in this regard, \nState and district-wise; \n(c)  whether the Government has not been able to  meet the target set by it for these five \nyears and if so, the reasons therefor; \n(d)  the steps taken by the Government to ensure  timely allotment of housing units to all \nthe stakeholders; \n(e)  the total number of beneficiaries who have been  covered under the PMAY -G, State-\nwise, district-wise and year-wise, particularly in the State of West Bengal; and \n(f)  the comparative study report of the States and  percentage of houses completed and \nallocated to beneficiaries and ongoing projects, State and district-wise? \n \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT  \n(SADHVI NIRANJAN JYOTI) \n \n \n(a)      The total number of houses constructed under Pradhan Mantri Awaas Yojana -Gramin \n(PMAY-G) during the last three years and the current year, State -wise is given in Annexure-\nI and particularly in Wardha and Amravati districts of Maharashtra is given in Annexure-II. \n  \nThe State/District -wise details of houses constructed under PMAY -G can be seen at the \nprogramme website at www.pmayg.nic.in--->AwaasSoft--->Reports--->Houses completed in \na financial year irrespective of target year. \n  \n  \n(b)        The target year -wise details of number of houses sanctioned and completed in \nParbhani, Dadra and Nagar Haveli & Daman and Diu during the last three years and current \nyear (as on 14.07.2022) are as under :- \n  \n\n  2019-20 2020-21 2021-22 2022-23 \n(as on 14.07.2022) \nSanctioned Completed Sanctioned Completed Sanctioned Completed Sanctioned Completed \nParbhani 996 776 1893 1278 6310 47 0 0 \nD&N Haveli \n& Daman \nand Diu \n0* 0 0* 0 52 0 0 0 \n*Targets equivalent to the available eligible beneficiaries from SECC, 2011 list were allocated to the \nUT(s) during the FY 2016 -17, 2017-18 and 2018-19. Therefore, the targets were not allocated during \nFY 2019-20 and 2020-21. A target of 1,182 houses has  been allocated out of the new Awaas+ survey \nduring FY 2021-22. \n  \nThe details of Central share released during last three years and the current year (as on \n14.07.2022) to the State of Maharashtra and UT of Dadra and Nagar Haveli, Daman and Diu \nare as under :- \n                                    (Rs. in crore) \n States/UTs 2019-20 2020-21 2021-22 2022-23 \nMaharashtra 1,815.32 1,310.10 1,249.79 0 \nD&N Haveli  & \nDaman and Diu \n55.98 0 0 0 \n  \nThe Central Assistance under PMAY -G is released to the State on the basis of balance \ntargets, liabilities and unspent balance available. These funds are released directly to the \nState/UT considering the State/UT as a unit. Further release of these funds to  beneficiaries in \nvarious districts is done by the respect State/UT Governments. The State -wise details of \nCentral share released during last three years and the current year (as on 14.07.2022) is given \nin Annexure-III. The district-wise details of fund releases under PMAY-G can be seen at the \nprogramme website at www.pmayg.nic.in---->AwaasSoft---->Reports---->High level \nfinancial progress report. \n  \n(c) & (d)  Under PMAY-G, out of the total target of 2.95 crore houses, a target of 2.70 crore \nhouses has already been allocated to the States/UTs, out of which 2.43 crore houses have \nbeen sanctioned, first installment of funds has been released to 2.33 crore beneficiaries and \n1.88 crore houses have been completed as on 14.07. 2022. During the period of Covid -19 \npandemic induced nation-wide lockdown, all construction activities including construction of \nhouses under PMAY-G were also affected which retarded the pace of construction of PMAY-\nG houses. Besides, the delays are also d ue to delay in release of Central & State Share from \nState Treasury to State Nodal Account of PMAY -G, cases of unwillingness of beneficiaries, \nmigration, disputed succession of deceased beneficiaries, delay in allotment of land to \nlandless beneficiaries by the States/UTs, at times General/Assembly/Panchayat elections, and \nunavailability of building materials. Therefore, the timeline for completion of PMAY -G has \nalso been extended from March, 2022 to March, 2024. The Ministry is taking the following \ninitiatives to ensure timely completion of the houses under PMAY-G:  \ni. Regular review of progress at the level of Ministry.   \nii. Launch of PMAY-G Dashboard for monitoring and supervision of the scheme.  \niii. Timely allocation of targets to the States/UTs and release of adequate funds.  \niv. Regular follow ups with the State to ensure release of central and state share of funds \nand provision of land to landless beneficiaries in rural areas.  \nv. Awards to the best performing States/UTs, Districts based on performance index \ndashboard, thereby creating healthy competition and motivation among the States/UTs \nfor achieving the set targets. \n \n \n\n  \n(e)        The details of number of beneficiaries sanctioned under the PMAY -G, State-wise and \nyear-wise and District -wise for the State of West Bengal are given in Annexure-IV. The \nDistrict-wise details of houses sanctioned under PMAY -G can be seen at the programme \nwebsite at www.pmayg.nic.in ---->AwaasSoft---->Reports---->Houses progress against the \ntarget financial year. \n  \n(f)        The State-wise detail of percentage of houses completed and allocated to beneficiaries \nand ongoing projects is given in Annexure-V. The district -wise details can be seen at the \nprogramme website at www.pmayg.nic.in ---->AwaasSoft---->Reports---->Houses progress \nagainst the target financial year. \n  \n***** \n  \n  \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \n \nAnnexure-I  \n   \nAnnexure-I referred to in reply to part (a) of Lok Sabha Unstarred Question No. 390 to \nbe answered on 19.07.2022 regarding Construction of Houses under PMAY-G  \n  \n \nYear-wise/State-wise details of number of houses constructed under PMAY -G during \nthe last three years and the current year (as on 14.07.2022): \n(Units in no.) \nS.No State Name \nTotal \ntarget \nallocated \n(From \ninception \n2016-17 till \ndate) \nHouses Constructed Total \nhouses \nconstructed \ntill date \nBalance \nhouses* \n2019-\n2020 \n2020-\n2021 \n2021-\n2022 \n2022-\n2023 \n1 \nArunachal \nPradesh 41,596 747 2,417 992 800 5,041 36,555 \n2 Assam 19,13,026 84,009 1,30,879 1,17,694 64,610 5,82,272 13,30,754 \n3 Bihar 38,83,611 3,76,216 9,42,615 5,08,363 1,52,066 25,89,223 12,94,388 \n4 Chhattisgarh 10,97,150 34,587 59,684 23,290 281 8,25,227 2,71,923 \n5 Goa 1,707 3 87 19 2 133 1,574 \n6 Gujarat 4,49,167 35,589 50,742 77,282 39,864 3,81,866 67,301 \n7 Haryana 30,789 6,670 1,215 263 203 20,989 9,800 \n8 \nHimachal \nPradesh 15,483 447 605 1,884 447 9,984 5,499 \n9 \nJammu and \nKashmir 2,01,633 5,610 21,745 42,569 6,577 91,493 1,10,140 \n10 Jharkhand 16,03,268 1,56,974 2,35,013 2,95,041 71,418 12,19,445 3,83,823 \n11 Kerala 42,212 779 686 2,440 366 20,28