6,854\n \n26\n \nPorbandar\n \n499\n \n628\n \n1\n \n7\n,\n182\n \n13,313\n \n27\n \nRajkot\n \n1,025\n \n921\n \n6\n \n41\n,\n628\n \n48,613\n \n28\n \nSabarkatha\n \n2,371\n \n208\n \n1\n \n23\n,\n299\n \n48,220\n \n29\n \nSurat\n \n3,683\n \n3,015\n \n20\n \n17\n,\n152\n \n50,302\n \n30\n \nSurendranagar\n \n1,790\n \n1,070\n \n2\n \n43\n,\n052\n \n51,567\n \n31\n \nTapi\n \n2,873\n \n3,360\n \n4\n \n38\n,\n726\n \n37,825\n \n32\n \nVadodara\n \n2,005\n \n629\n \n1\n \n37\n,\n747\n \n62,155\n \n33\n \nValsad\n \n1,969\n \n1,220\n \n6\n \n19\n,\n441\n \n57,534\n \nTotal \n \n \n50,460\n \n34,224\n \n100\n \n8\n,\n69\n,\n541\n \n17,11,885\n \n \n*****\n ",
    "ls|LS17_S9_U_399": " \n GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 399 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 19TH JULY, 2022/ASHADHA 28, 1944 (SAKA) \n \nNDRF FUNDS TO ASSAM \n \n399. SHRI PRADYUT BORDOLOI: \n \nWill the Minister of HOME AFFAIRS be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) the funds disbursed to the state of Assam under  National Disaster \nResponse Fund (NDRF) as on date in  light of the devastating floods \nexperienced this year in May and June 2022; \n \n(b) the funds disbursed under NDRF during the last  three years as on date, \nState/UT-wise and year-wise; \n \n(c) the amount sought under NDRF by the State  Government of Assam and \namount disbursed under  NDRF to the State during the last three years as on \ndate, year-wise; \n \n(d) whether allocation of funds to Assam under  NDRF in FY 2018-19 and 2019-\n20 has been on lower side and if so, the reasons therefor; and \n \n(e) whether the Government is considering to  increase central aid to Assam \nfor flood relief and  rehabilitation in view of the fact that the State faces  \nannual flooding four t imes more than the average suffered  by other States \nand if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n(SHRI NITYANAND RAI) \n(a) and (b):   The primary responsibility for disaster management rests with \nthe State Governments concerned. The State Governments undertake relief \nmeasures in the wake of natural disasters from the State Disaster Response \nFund (SDRF), already placed at their disposal , in accordance with the \nGovernment   of  India’s   approved   items  and  norms.    Additional  financial  \n\n--2-- \nL.S.US.Q.NO. 399 FOR 19.07.2022 \n \nassistance is  provided  from the  National Disaster Response Fund (NDRF), \nas per laid down procedure in case  of disaster of ‘severe nature’, which \nincludes an assessment based on the visit of an Inter -Ministerial Central \nTeam (IMCT). \nDuring the south -west monsoon of 2022, flood and landslides reported to \nhave caused extensive damages in various districts in the State of Assam. \nThe Central Government, without waiting for the Memorandum from the State \nGovernment of Assam, has constituted an IMCT on 19 th May, 2022, for on the \nspot assessment of damages.  IMCT has visited the State of Assam from 26 th \nto 29 th May, 2022 and second time from 30 th June to 3 rd July, 2022.  The \nCentral Government has released both the installments of SDRF of the year \n2022-23 totaling Rs 628.80 crore, in advance, to help the State Government of \nAssam to provide relief to the affecte d people. After receipt of the report of \nIMCT, the additional financial assistance is provided to the State \nGovernments from National Disaster Response Fund (NDRF), as per laid down \nprocedure. \n The State-wise details of releases from NDRF during 2018 -19 to 2022-23 \n(as on 12.07.2022) are given in Annexure. \n(c) and (d):    The allocation of SDRF to States is based on the \nrecommendations  of  successive  Finance Commissions, set up under Article  \n \n\n--3-- \nL.S.US.Q.NO. 399 FOR 19.07.2022 \n \n280   of   the  Constitution,   from   time   to  time.   Further,  the  disbursal   of  \nassistance from SDRF and NDRF is governed by the Guidelines and Items and \nNorms of assistance, which are available on Ministry of Home Affairs website \nhttps://ndmindia.mha.gov.in/images/gallery/Guidelines%20SDRF%20&%20NDR\nF.PDF and https://ndmindia.mha.gov.in/images/pdf/SDRF -NDRFNorms-\ndated8april2015.pdf, respectively. \nDetails of funds sought by Government of Assam and the funds released from \nNDRF during 2018-19 and 2021-22 are given as under:- \n(Rupees in crore) \n \n As per laid down guidelines, funds are released from NDRF subject to \nthe adjustment of 50% of balance available in the SDRF account of the \nState on 1 st April of the then financial year.  Hence, in these cases, \nwhere sufficient fund was available in SDRF, the net out go from the \nNDRF was either ‘NIL’ or reduced, against the approved amount. \n \n \nS.N. Financial Year Amount Sought by \nState Government \nAmount approved by \nHigh Level Committee \n(HLC) \nFunds Released \nfrom NDRF after \nadjustment  \n1. 2018-19 2,509.07 138.38 00.00* \n2. 2019-20 3,237.76 616.63 00.00* \n3. 2020-21 2,640.87 437.15 44.37* \n4. 2021-22 1,088.18 51.53 00.00* \n\n--4-- \nL.S.US.Q.NO. 399 FOR 19.07.2022 \n \nIn the memoranda, the State Government ha d projected damages beyond \nthe norms of NDRF and for inadmissible items.  Further, under NDRF, the fund \nis provided for immediate relief and not for long -term rehabilitation and \nreconstruction works, for which funds are generally sought by the State \nGovernment. This is the reason for variation in demands submitted by the \nState and the amount sanctioned by the Central Government. \n(e):  The recommendations of the 15 th Finance Commission (FC) on the \ndisaster risk management have been accepte d by the Central Government.  \nThe Commission had adopted a new methodology for state -wise allocations, \nwhich replaces the expenditure driven methodology. The new methodology is \na combination of capacity (as reflected through expenditure), risk exposure \n(area and population) hazard and vulnerably (risk index).  The Government \nhas significantly enhanced the allocation for all the States, including that of \nAssam under SDRF during the period of 2021 -22 to 2025 -26.  The allocation \nfor Assam under SDRF is Rs. 379 3.60 crore for the period 2021 -22 to 2025-26 \nas against Rs. 2541.00 crore during the period of 2015 -16 to 2019 -20. In \naddition, Rs. 948.40 crore has been allocated to the State of Assam under the \nState Disaster Mitigation Fund (SDMF).  \n****** \n \n \n\n \nL.S.USQ NO. 399 FOR 19.07.2022 \nANNEXURE \n \n \nSTATEMENT SHOWING STATE -WISE DETAILS OF RELEASE OF FUNDS UNDER NDRF \nDURING THE YEAR 2018-19 TO 2022-23 (AS ON 12.07.2022)   \n(Rs. in crore) \nSl.No. State Release from NDRF (For all calamities) \n2018-19 2019-20 2020-21 2021-22 2022-23 \n1. Andhra Pradesh 1004.88 570.91 657.03 351.43 -- \n2. Arunachal \nPradesh \n132.49 -- 59.34 -- -- \n3. Assam -- -- 44.37 -- -- \n4. Bihar -- 953.17 1255.27 1038.96 -- \n5. Chhattisgarh -- -- -- -- -- \n6. Goa -- -- -- -- -- \n7. Gujarat -- -- -- 1000.00 -- \n8. Haryana -- -- -- -- -- \n9. Himachal Pradesh 227.29 518.06 2.90 -- -- \n10. Jammu & Kashmir \n# \n-- -- -- -- -- \n11. Jharkhand -- -- -- 200.00 -- \n12. Karnataka 959.84 3208.28 689.27 1623.30 -- \n13. Kerala 2904.85 -- -- -- -- \n14. Madhya Pradesh 334.00 1712.14 1891.79 600.50 -- \n15. Maharashtra 2088.59 5189.40 420.12 1056.39 -- \n16. Manipur -- -- 26.53 -- -- \n17. Meghalaya -- -- 16.52 -- -- \n18. Mizoram -- -- -- -- -- \n19. Nagaland 195.99 176.52 1.34 -- 39.28 \n20. Odisha 341.72 3294.10 500.00 500.00 -- \n21. Punjab -- -- -- -- -- \n22. Rajasthan 526.14 1949.59 68.65 -- 13.46 \n23. Sikkim 54.93 -- 73.86 55.23 -- \n24. Tamil Nadu 900.31 -- 286.91 566.36 -- \n25. Telangana -- -- -- -- -- \n26. Tripura 171.74 -- 12.93 -- -- \n27. Uttar Pradesh 157.23 -- -- -- -- \n28. Uttarakhand -- -- -- -- -- \n29. West Bengal -- 958.33 2250.28 350.13 -- \nTOTAL 10000.00 18530.50 8257.11 7342.30 52.74 \n#  Now UT of Jammu and Kashmir and UT of Ladakh. \n \n****** \n \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S9_U_401": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nLOK SABHA\n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.\n \n401\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON \n19\nTH\n \nJULY\n, 2022\n \n \nESTIMATED FOOD GRAIN PRODUCTION\n \n \n \n401. SHRI VELUSAMY P.\n \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \nक ृ िष\n \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nकल्\n \nयाण\n \nमं\u0000ी\n \nbe pleased to state:\n \n \n(a) the estimated food grain production during the kharif season for the current year and for \nthe last year; \n \n(b) the\n \ndetails of estimated production of coarse cereals, pulses and oilseeds for the \ncurrent season; \n \n(c) the steps being taken by the Government to fill up the gap in shortfall if the production is \nless than average; \n \n(d) the impact on inflation due to shortfa\nll in food grains production; and \n \n(e) the steps being taken by the Government to control the rise in food prices due to \nshortfall in production?\n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \nक ृ िष\n \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nकल्\n \nयाण\n \nमं\u0000ी\n   \n \n \n \n(SHRI NARENDRA SINGH \nTOMAR)\n \n \n(\na\n)\n \nFor a\nn agriculture year\n \n(July\n-\nJune), the 1\nst\n \na\ndvance \ne\nstimates\n \n(kharif only)\n \nis\n \nreleased \nin the month of September.\n \nHence, it is \ntoo early to estimate kharif production of \nfoodgrains for \ncurrent\n \nyear 2022\n-\n23. However, as per the 3\nrd\n \nadvance estimates, 2021\n-\n22 \nthe kharif foodgrains production is estimated at 154.93 million tonnes. \n \n\n(\nb\n)\n \nt\no (d) \nDuring 2021\n-\n22, a\ns per 3\nrd\n \na\ndvance \ne\nstimates, \nthe \nestimated \nproduction of \ncoarse cereals, pulses and oilseeds \nis more than \nAverage production  of la\nst \n5 years\n \n(i.e. \n2016\n-\n17 to\n \n2020\n-\n21)\n \nas\n \ngiven in the table below:\n \n \n                                                                                         \n(\nProduction \nin Million Tonnes)\n \nCrops\n \n2021\n-\n22\n \n(3\nrd\n \nAdvance Estimates)\n \nAverage \n \n(\n2016\n-\n17 to 2020\n-\n21)\n \nC\noarse \nC\nereals\n \n50.70\n \n46.57\n \nP\nulses\n \n27.75\n \n23.82\n \nO\nilseeds\n \n38.50\n \n32.68\n \n \n \n(\ne\n)\n \nGovernment has taken various measures from time to time to \ncontrol the rise in\n \nfood \nprices\n \nwhich, inter\n-\nalia, include appropriately utilizing trade and fiscal policy instruments \nlike import duty, export restrictions, etc. to regulate domestic availability and moderate \nprices; advising States for effective action against hoarders and black marke\nters ; and, \nprovision of higher Minimum Support Prices to incentivize farmers for increasing \nproduction. Government is also implementing Schemes which, inter alia, include National \nFood Security Mission (NFSM), National Mission on Oilseeds and Oil Palm (NM\nOOP), \netc\n.\n \nfor increasing agricultural production and productivity through appropriate \ninterventions.\n \n*****\n \n. \n \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S9_U_402": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HEAVY INDUSTRIES  \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 402 \nANSWERED ON 19.07.2022 \n \nSUBSIDIES FOR PRODUCTION OF ELECTRIC VEHICLES \n \n402. SHRI PRATAPRAO JADHAV:  \nSHRI SANJAY SADASHIVRAO MANDLIK:  \nSHRI SHRIRANG APPA BARNE:  \nSHRI DHAIRYASHEEL SAMBHAJIRAO MANE:  \nSHRI MANOJ TIWARI:  \nSHRI BIDYUT BARAN MAHATO:  \nSHRI SUBRAT PATHAK:  \nSHRI SUDHEER GUPTA:  \nSHRI RAVI KISHAN:  \nSHRI SANGAM LAL GUPTA:  \nSHRI RAVINDRA KUSHWAHA:  \n \nWill the Minister of HEAVY INDUSTRIES भारी उद्योग मंत्री be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the conditions laid down by the Government for manufactures of Electric and Hybrid \nVehicles to qualify for subsidies under Faster Adoption and Manufacturing of Hybrid and \nElectric Vehicles in India (FAME-India) Scheme;  \n \n(b) whether it has come to the noti ce of the Government that many Electric Vehicles \n(EVs) manufacturers, who are availing subsidies under Electric Vehicle Promotion Scheme, \nare providing misleading information and if so, the details thereof;  \n \n(c) whether the Government proposes to make scr utiny process more stringent for the \nmanufacturers to qualify for the subsidies under the FAME India Scheme and if so, the steps \ntaken in this regard;  \n \n(d) whether the Government proposes to incentivise the local manufacturers through \nsubsidies, making India a hub for EVs and if so, the details thereof; and  \n \n(e) the other measures taken by the Government for value addition in EV manufacturing \nsector? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR HEAVY INDUSTRIES \n(SHRI KRISHAN PAL GURJAR) \n \n(a) to (c): The criteria for availing incentive under FAME -II ha ve been notified vide \nnotification S.O. 1300 (E) dated 08 th March, 2019, S.O. 1472 (E) dated 28 th March, 2019 and \nS.O. 2068 (E) dated 21 st June, 2019.   The copy of these notifications are annexed as \nANNEXURE. Further, copy of these notification s and other guidelines in this regard is \nuploaded on www.fame2.heavyindustries.gov.in .   The recognized testing agencies under rule \n126 of CMVR 1989 such as Automotive Research Association of India (ARAI), International  \n\n \n \nCentre for Automotive Technology  (iCAT) etc. are entrusted to certify that compliance to the \ncriteria as notified, which is regularly monitored by the Ministry of Heavy Industries. \n \n(d) & (e): Sir, the following steps have been taken by the Government to boost domestic \nmanufacturing of Electric vehicles: \ni. Government has approved the Production Linked Incentive (PLI) Scheme for \nAutomobile and Auto Components Industry in India to boost d omestic manufacturing \nof Advanced Automotive Technology products and attract investments in the \nautomotive manufacturing value chain with a budgetary outlay of Rs. 25,938 crores \nover a period of five years.  \nii. The Government on 12 th May, 2021 approved a Prod uction Linked Incentive (PLI) \nscheme for manufacturing of Advanced chemistry cell (ACC) in the country in order \nto bring down prices of battery in the country.  Drop in battery price will result in cost \nreduction of electric vehicles and increase in their sales. \niii. Under FAME India Scheme Phase -II, Phased Manufacturing Programme ( PMP) has \nbeen introduced with the  objective of domestic manufacturing of electrical vehicles, \nits assemblies/ sub -assemblies and parts/sub -parts thereby increasing the domestic \nvalue addition. \n******* \n \n \n \n\n1641  GI/2019 (1) \n \njftLVªh laö Mhö ,yö&33004@99  REGD. NO. D. L.-33004/99  \n \n \nvlk/kj.k  \nEXTRAORDINARY  \nHkkx II—[k.M 3 —mi&[k.M (ii) \nPART II—Section 3—Sub-section (ii) \nizkf/dkj ls izdkf'kr \nPUBLISHED BY AUTHORITY  \nla- 1164 ] ubZ fnYyh] lkseokj] ekpZ 11] 2019@iQkYxqu  20] 1940  \nNo.  1164] NEW DELHI, MONDAY,  MARCH  11,  2019/ PH ALGUNA 20,  1940  \n/uni092D/uni093E/uni0930/uni0940/uni0020/uni0909/g7378/uni094B/uni0917/uni0020/uni0914/uni0930/uni0020/uni0932/uni094B/uni0915/uni0020/uni