    "ls|LS17_S9_U_409": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 409 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 19TH JULY, 2022/ ASHADHA 28, 1944 (SAKA) \n \nTAMIL NADU REENACTED BILL FOR NEET \n \n409. SHRI S. VENKATESAN: \n \nWill the Minister of HOME AFFAIRS be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether the Government of Tamil Nadu re-enacted bill seeking the \nstudents from the State to be exempted from the National Entrance-cum-\nEligibility Test (NEET) has been forwarded to the Union Government for \napproval; and \n \n(b) if so, the status and progress thereof and the time by which this bill is \nlikely to be approved? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n(SHRI AJAY KUMAR MISHRA) \n(a) A Bill namely ‘The Tamil Nadu Admission to Undergraduate Medical \nDegree Courses Bill, 2021’, reserved by the Hon’ble Governor of Tamil \nNadu, for consideration and assent of the Hon’ble President of India was \nreceived in the Ministry on 02.05.2022 .  \n(b) As per practice, Bills reserved by the Hon’ble Governors of States for \nconsideration and assent of Hon’ble President of India are processed in \nthis Ministry in consultation with the nodal Central Ministries/Departments. \nAccordingly, the consultation process with nodal Central Ministries/ \nDepartments concerned with the Bill was initiated. Ministry of Health & \nFamily Welfare and Ministry of AYUSH furnished their ‘comments’ on the \nBill which have been shared with Government of Tamil Nadu on 21.06.2022 \nand 27.06.2022 respectively for their comments/clarifications. The \nconsultation process in such cases takes time and therefore no fixed time \ncan be prescribed for such approval. \n \n****** \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S9_U_410": "Government of India \nMinistry of Youth Affairs & Sports \nDepartment of Sports \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 410 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 19.07.2022 \nParticipation of Specially Abled Youth in Sports \n \n410.  DR. RAMAPATI RAM TRIPATHI: \nSHRI RANJEETSINGH HINDURAO NAIK \nNIMBALKAR: SHRI C.R. PATIL: \n SHRI SUDHAKAR TUKARAM SHRANGARE: \n SHRI PRATAP CHANDRA SARANGI: \n SHRI BRIJBHUSHAN SHARAN SINGH: \n SHRI P.P. CHAUDHARY: \n  \n Will the Minister of YOUTH AFFAIRS AND SPORTS be \npleased to state: \n \n(a) the steps currently taken/being taken by the Government to \nincrease international participation of India in sports for specially \nabledyouth; \n \n(b) whether the Government has taken any measures to enhance \nfacilities and promote participation of specially abled persons in \nsports competitions at  the international level including Special \nOlympic Games; \n \n(c) if so, the detailsthereof; \n \n(d) whether the Government is considering the need to build new \ndisabled-friendly sports stadiums or complexes to increase \nparticipation of specially abled youth in sports and to encourage \nthem in this field and if so, the details thereof; and \n \n(e) whether the Government has undertaken any measures to ensure \nequal recognition and recruitment opportunities to specially - abled \nsportspersons from all States and if so, the detailsthereof? \n \n \n\n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF YOUTH AFFAIRS & SPORTS \n[SHRI ANURAG SINGH THAKUR] \n \n(a) Government provides financial assistance to the Nationals \nSports Federations of Paralympic Committee of India (PCI), All India \nSports Council for Deaf (AISCD), Special Olympic Bharat (SOB) and \nCerebral Palsy Sports Federation of India (CPSFI) towards trainingof \nspecially abled athletes for their participation in international \nevents. The athletes selected for international competitions are \nprovided with all requi site facilities for their preparations including \nwholesome nutritious diet, food supplements, equipment support, \nstate-of-the-art infrastructure, lodging, travelling facilities, services \nof reputed Indian and Foreign Coaches/supporting staff, scientific & \nmedical support, sports kit, etc. Under Target Olympic Podium \nScheme (TOPS), talented Athletes are being selected and provided \nFinancial Assistance like Out of Pocket Allowances, equipment \nsupport, coaches etc. Athletes are being retained in the TOPS based \non the regular assessment of their performance. A total of 35 Para \nAthletes are being supported under TOPSScheme. \n(b) &(c) Yes. In addition to supporting the Special Athletes in their \nparticipation in the Special Olympic Games, the Government \nextends support in other international games as well. Sports \nAuthority of India has opened the regional sports centres of \nexcellence providing superior quality sports experiences to the \nyouth with disabilities. A Special Olympics Bharat National \nCoaching Camp was held in Floorball at the SAI centre ofexcellence \nin Sonipat wherein the special athletes availed all the facilities for \ntheir sportstraining. \n( d) Yes, to promote sports among the differently abled \nsportspersons, Sports Authority of India has established a Para \nCentre under SAI’s National Centre of Excellence (NCOE) Scheme at \nSAIRegionalCentre,Gandhinagar.Thecenterisoperationalinthe \n\ndisciplines of Athletics, Swimming, Powerlifting & Table Tennis. 46 \npara-athletes are undergoing training under qualified coa ches. All \nthe SAI Stadiums and training centres have been made disabled - \nfriendly and up -gradation of the same is carried out from time to \ntime to ensure comfort of differently -abled athletes. Under the \ncomponent “Promotion of Inclusiveness through Sports”  of Khelo \nIndia Scheme, sports infrastructure and training centers are created \nso as to ensure effective participation of persons with disabilities. \nDaulatabad stadium, Gurugram, has been assigned to carry out the \nSpecial Olympics sports and inclusive acti vations for the next 20 \nyears. \n( e) Yes. Recruitment of meritorious sportspersons including \nspecially-abled is done as per Government Guidelines. Further in the \nSports Authority of India, 05 posts at Assistant Coach Grade and 08 \nposts at Coach grade are reserve d for Paralympians for regular \nappointments. Currently, 04 Assistant Coaches, 01 Coach, 02 Senior \nCoach and 01 Chief Coach in the category of Paralympian (Specially- \nabled) are already working in the Sports Authority of India. More \nchances are given to the  recruitment opportunities of specially - \nabled sports persons in almost all States. Recently, Postal \nDepartment has recruited 06 Deaf Sports persons in Kerala and 06 \nin TamilNadu. \n \n********** ",
    "ls|LS17_S9_U_411": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF PANCHAYATI RAJ \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. †411 \nANSWERED ON 19.07.2022 \n \nFUNDS ALLOCATED UNDER BACKWARD AREAS \n \n†411. SHRI DILESHWAR KAMAIT:  \n          SHRI JYOTIRMAY SINGH MAHATO:  \n          MS. LOCKET CHATTERJEE: \n \nWill the Minister of PANCHAYATI RAJ be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether the Government has launched any scheme for the development and \nupgradation of basic infrastructure in the backward areas of the country; \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof; and \n \n(c) the details of funds allocated and utilized under the said scheme during the last three \nyears, State-wise? \n \n \nANSWER \n  \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR PANCHAYATI RAJ \n \n(SHRI KAPIL MORESHWAR PATIL) \n \n(a) to  (c): The Ministry of Panchayati Raj has not launched any scheme for infrastructure \ndevelopment and upgradation in the backward region of the country. However, grants under \nFifteenth Finance Commissions are released to the rural local bodies to, inter alia , utilise for the \nfelt needs under the 29 subjects enshrined in the XI schedule of the Constitution that include \ninfrastructure such as roads, culverts, bridges, ferries, waterways in rural areas of all region. \nPanchayats can create and maintain infrastructure purely out of the Finance Commission Grants or \nthrough convergence of other scheme such as Mahatma Gandhi National Rural E mployment \nGuarantee Scheme. Further, as informed by the Ministry of Rural Development, under Centrally \nSponsored Scheme of Pradhan Mantri Gram Sadak Yojana (PMGSY) all-weather road connectivity \nin rural areas catering to the access needs of the entire population of the target habitation is being \ncreated for which financial assistance is prov ided to the States. The roads constructed under \nPMGSY cater to all the strata of the society. Nevertheless, backward regions of the country viz. \nScheduled Tribal and backward districts have got specific focus in the scheme by giving special \ndispensation. The State/UT -wise funds released and utilis ed under the PMGSY scheme is at \nAnnexure.  \n*** \n \n \n \n \n\nAnnexure \nAnnexure Referred to in  Part (a) to (c) of the Lok Sabha Unstarred Question No. 411 \nRegarding Funds Allocated Under Backward Areas \nState-wise details of fund released and expenditure incurred under PMGSY scheme \nduring the last three years \n \nS1. \nNo. \n \nState/UT \nRelease of Central Share (Rs. in \ncrore) \nExpenditure Incurred \nincluding State share (Rs. in \ncrore) \n2019-20 2020-21 2021-22 2019-20 2020-21 2021-22 \n1 Andaman & \nNicobar \n0 0 9.215 0 3.78 5.45 \n2 Andhra Pradesh 476.27 53.2 50 338.84 396.75 508.81 \n3 Arunachal Pradesh 1123 952.31 1090.6 1270.03 1429.61 1279.06 \n4 Assam 2401.88 2516.62 1591.5 3628.93 2600.19 2488.02 \n5 Bihar 286.7 49.13 374.9975 1552.37 2173.52 1985.67 \n6 Chhattisgarh 1614.6 924.48 394.41 1159.7 1985.94 1902.2 \n7 Goa 0 0 0 0 0 0 \n8 Gujarat 0 79.08 195.5 29.35 133.32 400.16 \n9 Haryana 16.03 0 353.23 0.48 92.1 583.50 \n10 Himachal Pradesh 1284.89 745.24 517.45 724.34 1061 933.62 \n11 Jammu & Kashmir 695.5 1727.3 1328.34 1324.12 932.37 1483.95 \n12 Jharkhand 214.41 293.5 0 1312.94 1083.34 598.44 \n13 Karnataka 534.24 49.29 704.25 1.1 728.4 1488.47 \n14 Kerala 48.64 89.97 0 122.18 71.66 48.05 \n15 Madhya Pradesh 1308.97 1099.54 1392.13 1799.47 2166.99 2419.75 \n16 Maharashtra 150 0 0 207.12 212.09 372.65 \n17 Manipur 263.85 420.66 742 487.23 605.77 718.36 \n18 Meghalaya 357 355.29 483.92 289.79 473.71 522.32 \n19 Mizoram 576.06 1.59 74.34 285.18 277.32 332.82 \n20 Nagaland 88.89 72.89 145.31 70.8 144.7 124.27 \n21 Odisha 798.11 774.29 404.12 2489.91 1754.13 1791.15 \n22 Punjab 0 0 68.59 32.1 2.67 295.14 \n23 Puducherry 0 0 11.65 0 0 0.00 \n24 Rajasthan 184.74 237.15 917.50 289.11 492.13 1450.97 \n25 Sikkim 4.39 195.5 107.28 175.11 178.52 177.89 \n26 Tamil Nadu 308.46 265.38 440 799.29 626.92 1168.94 \n27 Telangana 267.38 0 86.63 289.44 288.59 410.4