    "ls|LS17_S9_U_431": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 431 \n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 19TH JULY, 2022/ ASHADHA 28, 1944 (SAKA) \n \nMODERNISATION OF POLICE FORCE SCHEME \n \n431. SHRI MARGANI BHARAT: \n \nWill the Minister of HOME AFFAIRS be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether it is not true that Andhra Pradesh is one of the performing \nStates under Modernization of Police Force Scheme (MPFS) during the last \nfew years; \n \n(b) whether it is also true that the Government grants incentives to better \nperforming States; \n \n(c) if so, the details of better performing incentives released to Andhra \nPradesh during the better performing years; and \n \n(d) the details of funds allocated for MPFS for 2021-22 and 2022-23, State-\nwise including Andhra Pradesh? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS \n(SHRI NITYANAND RAI) \n(a)  Under the scheme of ‘Assistance to States for Modernisation of \nPolice(ASMP)’(which was earlier known as ‘Modernization of State Police \nForces’ scheme), during the financial years 2018-19 and 2019-20, Andhra \nPradesh was one of  the States which had no unspent balance out of \nCentral releases.  \n \n\n-2- \nL.S.US.Q.NO. 431 FOR 19.07.2022  \n (b) and (c) The Government of India granted  incentives to better \nperforming States under this scheme till the year 2019-20. These \nincentives were granted to the better performing States out of the \nunreleased pooled funds. However, since 2020-21, these incentives could \nnot be released as substantial unspent balance remained with the State \nGovernments, including Andhra Pradesh. Better performance incentives of \nRs. 14.81 crore and Rs. 14.92 crore were released to the Government of \nAndhra Pradesh during the financial years 2018-19 and 2019-20 \nrespectively.  \n(d) State-wise details of funds allocated under this scheme during the \nfinancial years 2021-22 and 2022-23 are at Annexure. \n***** \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n1. \nANNEXURE \nLok Sabha Unstarred Qn. No. 431 for 19.7.2022 \n \nState-wise details of funds allocated during the financial years 2021-22 \nand 2022-23 under the scheme of ‘Assistance to States for Modernization \nof Police’ (renamed as ‘Assistance to States and Union Territories for \nModernization of Police’ on extending the scheme for UTs) \n(₹ in crore) \n  \nS. No. State 2021-22 2022-23 \n1. Andhra Pradesh 24.46 17.73 \n2. Arunachal Pradesh 3.92 3.26 \n3. Assam 26.40 11.64 \n4. Bihar 27.62 27.14 \n5. Chhattisgarh 9.72 11.01 \n6. Goa 1.03 2.79 \n7. Gujarat 25.58 26.67 \n8. Haryana 11.48 11.96 \n9. Himachal Pradesh 3.50 4.34 \n10. Jharkhand 9.21 11.87 \n11. Karnataka 38.37 19.19 \n12. Kerala 16.11 18.59 \n13. Madhya Pradesh 27.11 25.15 \n14. Maharashtra 47.11 36.24 \n15. Manipur 9.55 4.10 \n16. Meghalaya 3.75 3.29 \n17. Mizoram 4.77 2.74 \n18. Nagaland 10.74 3.57 \n19. Odisha 15.60 13.91 \n20. Punjab 16.42 11.18 \n21. Rajasthan 31.26 21.18 \n22. Sikkim 1.77 2.43 \n23. Tamil Nadu 34.84 44.04 \n24. Telangana 17.48 14.32 \n25. Tripura 7.84 3.91 \n26. Uttar Pradesh 63.19 56.62 \n27. Uttarakhand 3.37 5.43 \n28. West Bengal 28.90 23.49 \n29. Andaman & Nicobar -- 0.86 \n\n2. \n \n \n-------- \n \n \n \n30. Chandigarh -- 1.00 \n31 Dadra & Nagar Haveli and Daman & Diu -- 0.66 \n32. Delhi -- 10.69 \n33. Jammu & Kashmir -- 6.65 \n34. Ladakh -- 0.66 \n35. Lakshadweep -- 0.58 \n36. Puducherry -- 1.12 \n Sub-Total 521.10 460.00 \n Funds kept for  incentives for  Police \nReforms, etc. \n99.35 160.45 \n Grand Total 620.45 620.45 ",
    "ls|LS17_S9_U_432": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nLOK SABHA\n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.\n \n432\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON \n19\nth\n \nJULY\n \n2022\n \n \nADVANCE ESTIMATES OF AGRICULTURAL PRODUCTION\n \n \n432\n.    \nSHRI \nSUSHIL KUMAR \nSINGH\n \n \nWill\n \nthe\n \nMinister\n \nof\n \nAgriculture\n \nand\n \nFarmers\n \nWelfare\n \nक ृ िष\n \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nकल्\n \nयाण\n \nमं\u0000ी\n                    \nbe pleased \nto\n \nstate;\n \n(\na\n)\n \nthe details regarding the source of data for the Government towards arriving at \nthe advance estimates of agricultural \nproduction in the country;\n \n(\nb\n)\n \nthe method through which the Government arrives at the advance estimates;\n \n(\nc\n)\n \nthe different source for the agricultural statistics in the country;\n \n(\nd\n)\n \nthe steps being taken the Government to ensure that agricultural statistics \nbecome mor\ne advanced;\n \n(\ne\n)\n \nthe details of problem identified by the Government in the ongoing agricultural \nstatistics system?  \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n  \n \nक ृ िष\n \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nकल्\n \nयाण\n \nमं\u0000ी\n \n \n(SHRI NARENDRA SINGH TOMAR)\n \n \n(a) \nto\n \n(e\n)\n:\n \n \nGovernment of \nIndia has a statistically sound system of estimation of crop \narea, production and yield. For every ag\nricultural year (July\n-\nJune), \nDepartment\n \nof \nAgriculture and Farmers Welfare releases four Advance Estimates (AE) followed by \nFinal Estimates of production \no\nf identified \n28 major \nand 3 minor \nagricultural crops at \ns\ntate and national level\n.\n \nThe \ncrop production\n \nestimates at all\n-\nIndia level are prepared on \nthe basis of crop\n-\nwise data on area, production and yield furnished by State\n/UT\n \nGovernments. \nThe State/UT Gov\nernments\n \nprepare their crop production\n \nestimates\n \non \nthe basis of village wise data of crop \narea \nsown during \neach\n \nseasons \ncollected\n \nby \nPrimary workers\n \nof Revenue Department i.e. Patwari/Lekhpal\n \nas per\n \ntheir\n \nLand Record\ns\n \nmanual\ns\n.\n \n \n \nFor \ntimely reporting of\n \nAdvance Estimates\n \ndata by the State/UTs\n, \nthe De\npartment\n \nof Agriculture & Farmers Welfare\n \nimplements Centrally Sponsored Scheme namely \nImprovement of Agriculture Scheme (IAS)\n.\n \nUnder\n \nthe scheme, \nState\n/UT\n \nGovernments \nprovide data of crop \narea \ncoverage \non the\n \nbasis of\n \ncomplete enumeration of 20% \nvillages\n \nselected through statistical sampling\n \non priority basis. The yield of crops is \nestimated on the basis of Crop Cutting Experiments (CCEs)\n \nconducted\n \nunder General \nCrop Estimation Survey (GCES) \nby the states\n \nGove\nrnments\n.\n \n \n             \nGovernment also implements “Forecasting Agricultural output using Space                    \nAgro\n-\nmeteorology and Land based observations (FASAL)” scheme for the use of\n \nadvanced technologies like Remote Sensing, Agromet and Econometric\n \nmodels \nfor \ngeneration of crop production estimates \nto supplement the existing efforts. Further, \nsome\n \nstates \nhave\n \nalso\n \nstarted plot level data collection by using \nadvanced \ntechnologies.\n \n \n \n \n******\n ",
    "ls|LS17_S9_U_433": "1 \n \nO.I.H \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF FISHERIES, ANIMAL HUSBANDRY AND DAIRYING \nDEPARTMENT OF ANIMAL HUSBANDRY AND DAIRYING \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.433 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 19thJULY2022 \nPROMOTION OF DAIRY INDUSTRY \n433:  SHRI: SANJAY SETH \nWill the Minister of FISHERIES, ANIMAL HUSBANDRY AND DAIRYING क ृषि और कक सान क ल्याण मंत्री \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether there is any scheme for providing financial assistance for establishment or dairy in the country; \nand \n(b) the details of the steps taken to promote dairy industry in Jharkhand during the last three years and the \nnumber of people benefited there from, district-wise? \nANSWER \nTHE HON’BLE MINISTER OF FISHERIES, ANIMAL HUSBANDRY AND DAIRYING \n(SHRI PARSHOTTAM RUPALA) \n \n(a) Department of Animal Husbandry and Dairying is implementing following dairy development schemes, \nwherein provisions have been made for financial assistance to dairy cooperatives, milk producer \ncompanies etc, primarily for strengthening/creation of dairy infrastructure for quality milk procurement, \nits processing, marketing and working capital requirements: - \ni. National Programme for Dairy Development (NPDD) \nii. Dairy Processing and Infrastructure Development Fund (DIDF) \niii. Supporting Dairy Cooperatives and Farmer Producer Organisations engaged in dairy activities  \n(SDC&FPO) \n \n(b)  Details of the steps taken to promote dairy industry in Jharkhand during the last three years are as follows: \ni. Under NPDD scheme, Quality Milk Programme  project was approved with a total outlay of \nRs.821.57 lakh during 2019 -20 for implementation by Jharkhand State Cooperative Milk \nProducers' Federation Limited (JCMPFL) for strengthening of milk testing laboratories of Deoghar, \nKoderma, Latehar  and Ranchi dairy plants and for establishment of new State Central Lab at \nRanchi. \nii. Under SDC&FPO scheme, an amount of Rs.6.25 lakh of interest subvention was sanctioned on \nworking capital loan to JCMPFL in 2020-21. \niii. In addition, Government of Jharkhand has initiated a new scheme namely “Price incentive to Milk \nProducers” in financial year 2021 -22, wherein provision for price incentive at the rate Rs.1/Litre  \nwas made. This incentive has been revised to Rs.2/litre for the year 2022-23. This benefits milk \n\n2 \n \nproducers associated with the JCMPFL. District -wise number of Milk Producers' associated with \nJCMPFL and benefitted under the scheme is attached at Annexure. \niv. Besides above, Government of Jharkhand has sanctioned and released a sum of Rs. 7.00 crore for \nconstruction and establishment of new dairy plant at Palamu and a sum of Rs. 7.25 crore under \nRashtriya Krishi Vikas Yojna for dairy development activities in the State during 2020-21.  \nv. National Dairy Development Board (NDDB) is also providing management, technical and training \nsupport to JCMPCL. \n \n \n****  \n\n3 \n \n \nAnnexure \n \nNumber of Milk Producers’ associated with Jharkhand State Cooperative Milk Producers’ Federation \nLimited benefitted (as on 30.06.2022) \nS.No. District Number of Milk \nProducers \n1 Ranchi 7128 \n2 Lohardaga 3344 \n3 Bokarao 252 \n4 Ramgarh 258 \n5 Khunti 97 \n6 Gumla 88 \n7 Saraikela 13 \n8 Simdega 17 \n9 Dhanbad 99 \n10 West Singhbhum 21 \n11 Kodarma 65 \n12 Hazaribag 1556 \n13 Chatra 786 \n14 Deoghar 1043 \n15 Dumka 770 \n16 Godda 1037 \n17 Giridih 28 \n18 Jamtara 135 \n19 Latehar 861 \n20 Palamu 2241 \n21 Garhwa 1130 \n22 Sahibganj 1082 \n  Total 22051 \n \n**** ",
    "ls|LS17_S9_U_434": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF COOPERATION \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTIONNO. 434 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 19th JULY, 2022 \n \nFunds under CSISAC \n \n \n434. MS. MIMI CHAKRABORTY: \n Will the Minister of COOPERATION सहकारितामंत्री be please to state:  \n(a)  the details of new schemes to be rolled out under the Ministry for theyear 2022-23; \n(b)  whether there is any scope for increasing job opportunities in  cooperative sectors and \nif so, the details thereof; \n(c)  whether the funds have been allocated under the Central Sector Integrated Scheme on \nAgriculture Cooperation (CSISAC) for the promotion  and development of the cooperative \nsector across the country especially in West Bengal for the year 2022-23; and \n(d) if so, details thereof and if not, reasons therefor? \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF COOPERATION \nसहकारिता मंत्री (SHRI AMIT SHAH) \n(a) & (b):  In order to make 8.55 lakhs of Primary Cooperatives comprising of nearly 29 crore \nmembers in the Indian Cooperative Sector, economically vibrant, the Government has adopted \na multi-pronged approach by initiating various steps, of which some are listed below: - \nTo revitalize the cooperative sector, a centrally sponsored project “Computerization of \nPrimary Agriculture Credit Societies (PACS) ” has been approved by the Government, with a \nbudgetary outlay of Rs 2516 crores for digitalization of 63,000 functional PACS which are the \ngrassroot level economic entities of the three-tier rural credit structure. This project will bring \ntransparency and efficiency in their operations in a big way.   \n In order t o diversify the business activities of PACS and make them vibrant multi-\npurpose economic entities at Village/Panchayat level , draft model Bye -Laws are also being \nprepared in consultation with the State Governments, National Cooperative Federations and all \nother stakeholders  \n\n The Ministry is also formulating a new scheme titled “Cooperation to Prosperity” for \nall round development of cooperatives at all levels in consultation with all the stakeholders. \nFor modernizing and professionalizing the education and training in the cooperative sector , \nsteps are being taken t o reorient the training and educational Cooperative Institutions in \nconsultation with all the stakeholders, leading to the overall development of the vital sector of \neconomy resulting in creation of huge job opportunities as well. \n(c) & (d): The Central Sector Integrated Scheme for Agricultural Cooperation  (CSISAC) \nhas since been discontinued and an amount of Rs.50 crore has been earmarked in the budget \nallocation for the year 2022 -23 for committed liabilities under the Scheme  for all the States.  \nNational Cooperative Development Corporation  (NCDC) has provided the following \nassistance to the cooperatives in West Bengal in last 5 years under the CSISAC Scheme: - \nSl. \nNo. \nYear Disbursement in West \nBengal (Rs. in crore) \n1 2017-18 16.98 \n2 2018-19 329.34 \n3 2019-20 128.35 \n4 2020-21 59.13 \n5 2021-22 44.16 \n Total 577.96 \n \n****** ",
    "ls|LS17_S9_U_435": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF FOOD PROCESSING INDUSTRIES \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 435 \nANSWERED ON 19TH JULY, 2022 \n \nMEGA FOOD PARKS AND FOOD PROCESSING CLUSTERS UNDER PMKSY \n  \n435. SHRI NARANBHAI KACHHADIYA: \nSHRI PARBATBHAI SAVABHAI PATEL: \n  \n            Will the Minister of FOOD PROCESSING INDUSTRIES be pleased to state: \n \n  \n(a) the objectives and the current status of Pradhan Mantri Kisan Sampada Yojana \n(PMKSY); \n  \n(b) the manner by which the setting up of Mega Food Parks and Food Processing Clusters \nis encouraged under the Yojana; and  \n  \n(c) the details of approved Mega Food Parks and Food Processing Clusters under PMKSY, \nState-wise including Gujarat? \n  \nANSWER \n  \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR FOOD PROCESSING INDUSTRIES \n(SHRI PRAHLAD SINGH PATEL) \n  \n(a): The ‘Pradhan Mantri Kisan SAMPADA Yojana (PMKSY)’ has been envisaged as a \ncomprehensive package for creation of modern infrastructure with efficient supply chain \nmanagement from farm gate to retail outlet. It will not only provide a boost to the growth of \nfood processing sector in the country but also help in providing better returns to farmers and a \nstep towards doubling of farmers income, creating employment opportunities especially in the \nrural areas, reducing wastage of agricultural produce, increasing the p rocessing level and \nenhancing the export of the processed foods. \n  \nA total of 853 projects for food processing have been approved in the 30 different States \nof this country so far with total project cost of Rs.21058.29 crores, with the aim to create \nprocessing capacity of 216.81 Lakh MT/annum, preservation capacity of 70.014 Lakh \nMT/annum, employment of 10,61,361 persons and providing benefits to 41,42,917 farmers.   \n  \n(b): The Ministry encourages setting up of food processing facilities/units by extending \nfinancial assistance as grants-in-aid to the approved projects, application for which are invited \nthrough Expression of Interest (EoI) from time to time, as the sub -schemes of PMKSY, \nincluding Mega Food Park (MFP) and Agro Processing Cluster (APC) are dema nd driven \nContinuation of Agro Processing Cluster (APC), Creation/Expansion of Food Processing & \nPreservation Capacity (CEFPPC), Integrated Cold Chain & Value Additional Infrastructure  \n(CCI); Food Testing Labs (FTL) and Operation Greens (OG) during 15th Finance Commission \ncycle has only been approved by the Government.  \n  \n(c): The State-wise details of projects, including Gujarat, approved under MFP, APC, and \nOG sub-schems of PMKSY are at Annexure. \n       \n***** \n  \n\n \nANNEXURE \n \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (c) OF LOK SABHA UNSTARRED \nQUESTION NO. 435 FOR ANSWER ON 19TH JULY, 2022 REGARDING MEGA FOOD \nPARKS AND FOOD PROCESSING CLUSTERS UNDER PMKSY \n  \nDetails of project approved under MFP, APC and OG sub-schemes under PMKSY \nProjects approved under MFP Scheme \n \nSr. \nNo. State/UT SPV/ IA Name \nDate of \nFinal \nApproval \n  \nProject \nCost \n(INR in \nCr) \n  \nAmount of \ngrant \napproved \n(INR in Cr) \n  \nAmount of \ngrant \nreleased \n(INR in Cr) \n1. \nAndhra  \nPradesh \n(3 Projects) \n  \nSrini Food Park Pvt. Ltd., Chittoor 30.03.2009 121.1 50 49.92 \n2. Godavari Mega Aqua Food Park Pvt. \nLtd., West Godavari 16.12.2013 122.6 50 44.53 \n3. \nAndhra Pradesh Industrial \nInfrastructure Corporation (APIIC), \nKrishna \n31.12.2015 112.94 50 33.64 \n4. \n Arunachal  \nPradesh \n(1 Project) \nRongoge Mega Food Park, Pvt.  Ltd, \nPapum Pare 12.09.2019 73.02 43.25 12.02 \n5. Assam \n(1 Project) \nNorth East Mega Food Park Ltd., \nNalbari 30.03.2009 84.96 48.84 48.84 \n6. Bihar \n(2 Projects) \nPristine Mega Food Park Pvt. Ltd., \nKhagaria 06.08.2014 120.13 41.9 34.81 \n7. Bihar Industrial Area Development \nAuthority (BIADA), Muzaffarpur 07.04.2022 180.57 45.40 - \n8. Chhattisgarh \n(1 Project) \nIndus Best Mega Food Park Pvt. Ltd., \nRaipur 04.06.2014 96