    "ls|LS17_S9_U_464": " \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 464 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 20.07.2022 \n \n \nKALAMKARI TRADITIONAL BLOCK PRINTING \n \n \n \n464. SHRI BALASHOWRY VALLABHANENI: \n \n \nWill the Minister of TEXTILES वस्त्र मंत्री \nbe pleased to state : \n \n(a)whether the Government proposes to preserve and promote Kalamkari Traditional Block Printing \ncraft in Krishna District of Andhra Pradesh;  \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof; and  \n \n(c) if not, the support provided by the Government to preserve and promote Kalamkari Traditional \nBlock Printing craft in Krishna District of Andhra Pradesh? \n \n \nउत्तर \nANSWER \nवस्त्र राज्‍ य मंत्री (श्रीमती दर्शना जरदोर्) \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR TEXTILES  \n (SMT. DARSHANA JARDOSH) \n \n(a) to (c): The office of Development Commissioner (Handicrafts) has taken the following steps to \npreserve and promote Kalamkari traditional Block Printing craft under National Handicrafts \nDevelopment Programme (NHDP) in Krishna District of Andhra Pradesh: - \n \nI. Under Sk ill Development in Handicraft Sector Scheme: 2 Integrated Design Development \nProgrammes (IDDP), 2 Design Development Workshops (DDW), 1 Toolkit distribution \nProgramme and 3 Handicraft Technical Training Programmes (HTTP) have been held.  \n \nII. Under Ambedkar Hastshilp Vikas Yojana: one  Producer Company has bee n formed and need \nbased support under Design & Technical Upgradation Scheme, Marketing Support & Services \nand Direct Benefit to Artisans have been given to the company for its sustainable development. \n*** \n \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S9_U_465": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 465 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 20.07.2022 \n \nASSETS LEASED UNDER NMP \n \n465.   SHRIMATI CHINTA ANURADHA: \n    \n    Will the Minister of RAILWAYS be pleased to state: \n(a)   the details of each of the assets that are going to be leased under \nNational Monetization Pipeline(NMP) in Konkan Railway and dedicated \nFreight Corridor assets; \n(b)   the details of annual revenue that the Government at present is able \nto yield through the above identified assets; \n(c)  the amount of revenue likely to be gained once identified assets are \nleased; \n(d)  whether the leaseholder will continue with reservation policy in jobs; \nand \n(e)  if not, the manner in which Ministry is going to ensure that there \nwould not be any retrenchment and reservation policy remains intact? \n \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF RAILWAYS, COMMUNICATIONS AND \nELECTRONICS & INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY \n(SHRI ASHWINI VAISHNAW) \n \n \n(a) As per National Monetization Pipeline Plan prepared by NITI Aayog , \nmonetization of 741 Kms of Konkan Railways Track and 673 Kms{20% of \ntotal  Dedicated Freight Corridor(DFC) network}  of DFC track and allied \ninfrastructure is included. \n(b) The revenue realized from train operations over 741 Kms of Konkan \nRailway Track  in Financial Year 2021 -22 was ` 1167.65 Crores. No \nspecific assessment of revenue from DFC can be made since it is only \npartly operational. \n\n-2- \n \n(c) At present, it is difficult to estimate revenue for Konkan Railways in \ncase of leasing(if any) in future. The details of revenue likely to be \nyielded from DFC assets can only be worked out after full DFC line is \nmade operational. \n(d) & (e) Do not arise. \n \n***** \n \n  ",
    "ls|LS17_S9_U_466": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF MINES \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.  466   \nANSWERED ON 20.07.2022 \n \nDISPLACED PEOPLE IN ODISHA \n \n466.  SHRI JUAL ORAM: \n \nWill the Minister of MINES be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government has reviewed the problems of the people in Odisha who \nhave been displaced by the projects of National Aluminium Company Limited \n(NALCO) and Mahanadi Coal Fields Limited (MCL) in Odisha; \n(b) if so, the number of such displaced families; and \n(c) the details of the steps taken to resolve their problems? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF MINES, COAL AND PARLIAMENTARY AFFAIRS \n(SHRI PRALHAD JOSHI) \n \n(a): The problems of the people of Odisha who have been displaced by the projects \nof National Aluminium Company Limited (NALCO) are being periodically reviewed by \nthe Rehabilitation and Periphery Development Advisory Committees (RPDAC) \nconstituted by Government of Odisha. In Mahanadi Coalfields Ltd. (MCL), \ncompensation against acquisition of land, trees/ crops and structures is assessed as \nper Right to Fair Compensation and Transparency in Land Acquisition, Rehabilitation \nand Resettlement Act, 2013 (RFCTLARR Act, 2013) and being paid to the villagers after \nvesting of land with MCL under The Coal Bearing Areas (Acquisition and Development) \nAct 1957 (CBA (A&D) Act, 1957). \n \n(b) & (c): A total number of 635 persons were displaced by project activities in \nNALCO. Out of which, 600 persons were displaced in Damanjodi, Koraput District and \n35 persons were displaced at Angul District. Out of 600 Land displaced persons (LDP) \nat Damanjodi, 599 LDPs/ their nominees were employed in NALCO. Regarding balance \none case, decision is awaited from District Authority due to non -finalization of the \nnominee because of their fa mily dispute. Out of 35 LDPs at Angul, 34 LDPs/their \nnominees were employed at NALCO while one LDP had preferred for one -time cash \nassistant in lieu of employment . In MCL about 16,297 persons have been identified to \nbe displaced, out of which 11,837 have a lready been provided resettlement benefits. \nBut till date out of 11,837 families, only 8,248 families have shifted from their respective \nvillages. Out of 11,837 families, 3,455 families have been provided with resettlement \nplot at Resettlement and Rehabilitation site and 8,382 families have been provided with \nCash in lieu of plot till date. Resettlement benefits for balance families are under \nprocess. The resettlement benefits are provided as per Rehabilitation and Resettlement \n(R&R) Policy of Government of Odisha. \n \n***** ",
    "ls|LS17_S9_U_467": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF COMMERCE & INDUSTRY \nDEPARTMENT FOR PROMOTION OF INDUSTRY AND INTERNAL TRADE \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 467. \nTO BE ANSWERED ON WEDNESDAY, THE 20TH JULY, 2022. \n \nPROMOTION OF FOREIGN INVESTMENT \n \n467.  SHRIMATI MALA RAJYA LAXMI SHAH: \nSHRI KUNWAR PUSHPENDRA SINGH CHANDEL: \n \nWill the Minister of COMMERCE AND INDUSTRY be pleased to state: \n                                    वाणिज्य  एवं उद्योग मंत्री \n(a)  whether the Government is making special efforts  to promote foreign  \ninvestment; \n \n(b)  if so, the details thereof; \n \n(c)  whether the Government also proposes to make  fresh institutional reforms to \nattract foreign investment; \n \n(d)  if so, the details thereof; and \n \n(e)  the details of foreign investment made during the last two years? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nवाणिज्य  एवं उद्योग मंत्रालय में राज्य मंत्री (श्री सोम प्रकाश)   \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF COMMERCE & INDUSTRY \n(SHRI SOM PARKASH) \n \n(a) to (d):    To promote Foreign Direct Investment (FDI), the Government has put in \nplace an investor -friendly policy, wherein most sectors, except certain \nstrategically important sectors, are open for 100% FDI under the \nautomatic route. Further, the policy on FDI is reviewed on an ongoing \nbasis, to ensure that India remains attractive & investor friendly \ndestination. Changes are made in the policy after having consultations \nwith stakeholders including apex industry chambers, associations, \nrepresentatives of industries/groups and other organizations. In the \nrecent past, reforms in the FDI policy have been undertaken in sectors \nsuch as Insurance, Petroleum & Natural Gas, Telecom etc. \n  \nGovernment has launched 'Make in India' initiative to facilitate \ninvestment, foster innovation, build best in class infrastructure and make \nIndia a hub for manufacturing, design and innovation. Further, \ninvestment outreach activities are done through Minis tries, State \n\nGovernments and Indian Missions abroad for enhancing international \ncooperation and promoting foreign investment in the country. \n  \n(e):     The foreign investment reported through Foreign Direct Investment \ninflow is as under:  \n \nS. No. Financial Year Total FDI Inflow \n(in USD million) \nTotal FDI Inflow \n(in INR crore)* \n1. 2020-21 (P) 81,973 6,08,340 \n2. 2021-22 (P) 83,676 6,22,428 \n  \nSource: Reserve Bank of India.  (P) – Figures are provisional. \n* As per yearly conversion rate as calculated from the monthly average \nexchange rate provided by RBI. \n  \n****** \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S9_U_468": "1 \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF COMMUNICATIONS   \nDEPARTMENT OF TELECOMMUNICATIONS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 468 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 20TH JULY, 2022 \n \nMOBILE NETWORK COVERAGE \n \n468.  ADV. DEAN KURIAKOSE:  \n \nWill the Minister of COMMUNICATIONS be pleased to state:  \n \n(a)  whether the Government has the details of tribal hamlets in the Idukki Parliamentary \nConstituency that has no mobile network coverage;  \n \n(b)  if so, the details thereof;  \n \n(c)  whether the Government has any plans to use the funds earmarked for tribal development \nto increase the mobile network coverage of these areas; and  \n \n(d)  if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR COMMUNICATIONS  \n(SHRI DEVUSINH CHAUHAN) \n \n(a) & (b)  As per information from field units of Department of Telecommunications (DoT), \nin Idukki Parliamentary Constituency (Ernakulum and Idukki districts) out of 173 villages, 163 \nvillages are covered and 10 villages (74 numbers of Haml ets), are reported to be uncovered with \nmobile connectivity. \n \n(c) & (d)  Government is implementing various schemes under Universal Service Obligation \nFund (USOF). To enhance telecom connectivity in the rural and predominately tribal areas of the \ncountry inclu ding North Eastern Region (NER) and areas affected by Left Wing Extremism \n(LWE), Aspirational Districts and Islands, around 9960 mobile towers have been planned under \nUSOF.  Further t he scope of BharatNet project has been recently extended up to all inhabited \nvillages beyond Gram Panchayats (GPs) in the country. \n \n \n \n******** \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S9_U_469": "LOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 469 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 20.07.2022 \n \n \n \n \nEXPORT OF RAW JUTE \n \n \n469. SHRI SRIDHAR KOTAGIRI: \n \n \n \n \n \nWill the Minister of TEXTILES वस्त्र मंत्री \nbe pleased to state : \n \n \n(a) the details of export data of raw jute with prices for the last five years;  \n \n(b) the acres of land cultivated for jute, State-wise for last five years;  \n \n(c) the year -wise price per jute bag and High Density Polyethylene (HDPE) bag by Food Corporation of \nIndia (FCI) for the last five years;  \n \n(d) the details of jute produced, cultivated and procured, State-wise;  \n \n(e) the total number of HDPE plastic bags made in India and State-wise data of the same; and  \n \n(f) the transportation price of jute from West Bengal to Andhra Pradesh? \n \n \n \n \nउत्तर \nANSWER \nवस्त्र राज्‍ य मंत्री (श्रीमती दर्शना जरदोर्) \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR TEXTILES  \n (SMT. DARSHANA JARDOSH) \n \n(a):   The Export data of raw jute with its prices for the last five years is at Annexure-I. \n \n(b) & (d): The details of land cultivated, jute produced and procured at MSP are at Annexure-II. The raw \njute is not procured by the State. It is procured by traders on the market price and supply to Jute Mills. In \naddition to, Jute Corporation of India Ltd. has the mandate to purchase raw jute whatever quantity is \noffered by the farmers at MSP.  \n \n(c): Jute Commissioner declares provisional price of B -Twill jute bags of 50 kg capacity on monthly basis. \nThe details of jute bag price of the last five years are at Annexure-III. The price of High Density \nPolyethylene (HDPE) bag by Food Corporation of India (FCI) for the last five years is at Annexure-IV. \n \n(e): No such data is maintained.  \n \n(f):   M/s Jute Balers Association and M/s A.M. Mair & Co Pvt. Ltd. publish the quotation price of raw jute \nincluding transportation charges for Ex -South Bengal and Others for procurement by jute mills. No \nseparate data for tr ansportation price of raw jute from West Bengal to other states including Andhra \nPradesh is maintained. \n \n\n \nAnnexure-I in reply to part (a) of the Lok Sabha Unstarred Q.No. 469 for 20.07.2022  regarding \nExport of Raw Jute asked by Shri Sridhar Kotagiri \nExport data of raw jute with prices for the last 5 years  \nExport of Raw Jute form India   \nYear Qty ('000 M Ton) Value (Rs. Million)   \n2017-18 27.2 955.2   \n2018-19 24.07 1,087.3   \n2019-20 21.87 1,072.12   \n2020-21 30.56 1,916.76   \n2021-22 \n(April-Feb) 29.55 2,051.4   \nSource - Directorate General of Commercial Intelligence & Statistics (DGCIS), Kolkata. \n \n \n \n \n \n\nAnnexure-II in reply to part (b) & (d)  of the Lok Sabha Unstarred Q.No . 469 for 20.07.2022 regarding Export of Raw Jute asked by Shri \nSridhar Kotagiri \n \n \nStates \n \nCultivated Area(‘000 Hectares)                                                                                       Production('000Balesof180Kgs.each) \n  \n2016-17 \n \n2017-18 \n \n2018-19 \n \n2019-20 \n \n2020-21 \n \n2016-17 \n \n2017-18 \n \n2018-19 \n \n2019-20 \n \n2020-21 \n \nAssam \n \n75.14 \n \n69.95 \n \n65.79 \n \n64.25 \n \n65.00 \n \n802.75 \n \n841.29 \n \n761.69 \n \n791.67 \n \n778.19 \n \nBihar \n \n91.38 \n \n83.47 \n \n70.63 \n \n48.39 \n \n42.48 \n \n1,356.00 \n \n1,109.71 \n \n928.84 \n \n646.81 \n \n780.43 \n \nMadhya Pradesh \n \n6.00 \n \n6.00 \n \n0.00 \n \n0.00 \n \n0.00 \n \n5.00 \n \n9.00 \n \n0.00 \n \n0.00 \n \n0.00 \n \nMeghalaya \n \n6.67 \n \n6.67 \n \n6.68 \n \n6.68   \n68.37 \n \n68.47 \n \n68.52 \n \n68.54  \n \nNagaland \n \n3.07 \n \n3.08 \n \n3.08 \n \n3.08   \n5.85 \n \n32.61 \n \n32.61 \n \n32.61  \n \nOdisha \n \n0.75 \n \n0.95 \n \n0.20 \n \n0.15 \n \n0.76 \n \n1.26 \n \n13.15 \n \n1.73 \n \n1.81 \n \n10.15 \nTripura  \n0.59 \n \n0.55 \n \n0.66 \n \n0.62   \n5.37 \n \n5.00 \n \n5.84 \n \n \n3.17 \n \n0.00 \nWest Bengal  \n522.47 \n \n515.08 \n \n518.26 \n \n505.23 \n \n506.16 \n \n8,187.66 \